{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,210p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-16 10:52:30 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-16 17:52:30 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a143f7-0693-7a32-9229-1fccbcc526a9\n# Task: plan v2.1 — the mob's reconciled amendments applied to Fable's 6d plan v2\nRole: Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: gpt-6-astra | Assigned: 2026-10-16 18:45 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: owner commissioned v2.1 from the mob's sharpest reviewer; delivered as the resumed turn of its mob-review thread (final message promoted verbatim below; the run's stdout is collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21-amend-codex.gpt-6-astra.stdout3.log — two earlier attempts failed on codex-exec-resume flag rejections, quirk recorded in docs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md). Pending: glm-5.3 confirmation, then Fable's diff-confirm before any merge.\n\nI’ve checked the restore consumers and the exception path. Completed outcomes need both a delivery state and a completion descriptor, because link wakes discard request fields that pending-wait validation requires. For tenure loss, I’ll specify stage-local catches that return the latest completed state; rebinding inside `run-cycle` cannot update the outer catch’s argument.\n\n# Slice 6d plan v2.1: reconciled mob amendments\n\nRole: Architect. Baseline: Fable’s `1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md`. Governing reconciliation: `1792143479000-orchestrator-6d-plan-v2-mob-consensus.md`. Verification tree: `c6bc91f7`, including module-pins commit 2.\n\nThis is an amendment edition: the replacement blocks below supersede the named v2 sections or clauses completely. Unmentioned text remains unchanged. Section numbers are retained. Historical execution claims in v2 remain attributed to Fable; this revision performed source inspection only, ran no tests, and changed no files.\n\nThe owner ratified defaults 1–5 and 7 unchanged, and defaults 6 and 8 with the amendments written into §3.10 below. The three P1 amendments all belong in 6d-a.\n\n**Replacement for the opening summary’s affected claims:**\n\n1. The task remains a product: active control, ordered outstanding waits, ordered completed outcomes, parked records, stores and installs. Completed outcomes cover stream, FFI, link and install wakes, including terminal outcomes. They are not validated as outstanding waits.\n2. `run-fuel` performs one budgeted execution slice or scheduler round, followed by at most one bridge step. It never restarts the runner inside the same call. Child execution receives the same bounded entry point.\n3. The closed-gate guard ships in 6d-a with the first running fenced restore. Direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` paths are covered.\n4. Tenure failure is handled at stage boundaries with an explicit state-bearing outcome. The newest completed machine is retained for inspection, never adopted without authority; recovery remains the last durable checkpoint.\n5. Ready cursor updates carry independently emitted cell bodies under the task census’s canonical cell identities. Completed operation IDs receive shape validation separately from retained-write custody validation.\n6. Module-pins commit 2 has landed. Implementers locate sites by symbol and quoted text, not by v2’s obsolete offset estimate.\n7. Order remains 6d-a, then 6d-b and 6e on separate branches. 6d-a supplies the carrier, bounded runner and fencing; 6d-b supplies holder scheduling and migration lifecycle. The complete holder invariant is not claimed before 6d-b.\n8. The effort figures remain estimates, not deadlines or review-round limits. Acceptance gates determine completion.\n\n**Change:** Incorporates consensus P1 items 1–3, P2 items 4–8, and citation/sequencing hygiene.\n\n## 1. The nine findings, verified\n\n**Replacement for the section’s interpretation of readiness:**\n\nThe nine findings against v1 remain accepted. Fable’s reproduced cases and source observations remain historical evidence. V2’s proposed answers required the reconciled amendments below: completed link/install outcomes, bounded bridge execution, immediate fencing, explicit exception-state transport, correct cursor-update encoding, corrected acceptance equations and complete ready-value traversal.\n\nNo v2 assertion that link wakes restore directly, that unchanged `maybe-run` bounds a fuelled invocation, or that local `assoc` updates an outer catch’s state is normative in v2.1.\n\n**Change:** Preserves the original findings while superseding mechanisms rejected by the mob.\n\n## 2. What v1 got wrong, plainly\n\nRetain v2 §2. Append:\n\n12. **V2’s remaining execution gaps.** The bridge loop could restart the budget, and the gate guard was scheduled after running fenced states became available.\n13. **V2’s completed-outcome gap.** Link and install completions already enter the queue, after their outstanding-operation resources may have been retired.\n14. **V2’s mechanical corrections.** Cursor identities and update bodies require separate treatment; completed operation IDs need shape-only validation; the ready value participates in the portability oracle; stage-local state must cross failure handling explicitly; ordered operand multiplicity is not set cardinality.\n\n**Change:** Records the reconciled amendments without rewriting v1’s history.\n\n## 3. Plan v2.1\n\n### 3.1 State model: the task-state product\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.1 completely.**\n\n**Task**, root or install child:\n\n```text\ntask = (kind,\n        run?,\n        waits = [wait …],\n        ready = [ready …],\n        parked-table, result?, parked-id?,\n        store, module-stores, cursor-cells, free-env, id-counter,\n        requires, code, installs, custody)\n```\n\n| Kind | Machine classification | Waits | Run | Ready | Parked-id | Result |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|\n| `:running` | `:control` set | 0..n | exactly one | 0..n | absent | absent |\n| `:parked` | control nil; value names a held parked record | 0..n | none | 0..n | table key | absent |\n| `:blocked` | control nil; not parked; waits or ready nonempty | 0..n | none | 0..n | absent | absent |\n| `:halted` | control nil; halted; waits and ready empty | none | none | none | absent | present |\n\nA halted activation beside queued work classifies `:blocked`: the scheduler processes the queue before returning the halt result. A halted activation with outstanding waits and an empty queue also classifies `:blocked`, dropping its halt value as today. The latter is an existing carrier normalization, not a claim that the source halt value is unobservable; acceptance pins it separately.\n\n**Row grammar.** Task slots 0–16 retain their meanings; slots 17 and 18 remain separate ordered references.\n\n```clojure\n:run\n[:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]\n\n:ready\n[[:ref :act :tail]          ; delivery state, without an outstanding pending\n [:ref :completion]        ; completed-operation descriptor\n :V                        ; delivered value, including [:lit nil]\n :optional-D               ; nil or status keyword\n [:ref :cursor-cells]      ; resource updates, not another census\n :optional-V]              ; raised refusal data, absent unless required\n\n:completion :completion    ; discriminated by completion-table\n\n:task\n[...                       ; slots 0–16 unchanged\n [:refs :run]              ; slot 17\n [:refs :ready]]           ; slot 18\n```\n\n`:completion` is a new discriminated structural row. Its descriptor table is:\n\n| Completion tag | Slots after the tag | Restored engine meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:next` | `C` | Ordinary completed reader; reconstruct cursor-ref and stream-id through the census. |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref, datom-V, optional-op-id-D` | Ordinary completed append outcome; never append again. |\n| `:ffi` | `C, call-id-D, op-D` | Completed FFI response reader; restore engine reason `:next` and correlation fields. |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D` | Completed request append; restore `:request-sent true`, so successful resume creates the response wait rather than delivering the append result to the guest. |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D` | Completed link-request outcome; no outstanding request or response transport is required. |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D` | Completed link-response outcome. |\n| `:install` | `module-S, try?-D` | Completed initializer outcome; no remaining install entry is required. |\n\nThe existing `pending-table` remains the grammar of **outstanding** operations. `completion-table` is distinct. The `:ffi` and `:ffi-request` wire tags preserve the existing distinction between engine `:next` and `:put` entries carrying FFI correlation.\n\nAll existing queue producers are covered. `wake-installed` queues install completions, and `poll-links` queues link outcomes. A completion must not reconstruct a retired request envelope, response cursor or install solely to satisfy pending validation.\n\nFor link/install success, status may be absent: it is encoded canonically as nil. A try failure travels in `:V` as ordinary returned outcome data. An ordinary require failure carries status `:link-refused` and its refusal value in the last slot; restore reinstates `:refusal` so the existing terminal-resume path raises the same error. The refusal slot is absent for other outcomes. Status is preserved, not inferred from the delivered value.\n\nThe redundant queue-entry `:cursor` is not carried. Its operative resource updates are carried explicitly. Empty or nil update maps have one spelling: an empty update row.\n\n**Frame and completion validation.**\n\n| Rule | Requirement | Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | States are `:act`, `:tail`, `:run`; a run has exactly state, segment, pc, env, window and k. | `:frame-shape` |\n| C2 | Exact integer PC; act and return-frame PCs are positive and in bounds; run PC may be zero. | `:pc-kind`, `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | Act and return frames retain body continuity; the run itself skips that predecessor check. | `:pc-body` |\n| R1 | Run window keys are in the body’s register scope. | `:window-scope` |\n| R2 | Run window keys equal `L(pc)`; act/return windows retain `saved(pc, rd)`. | `:window-extra`, `:window-missing` |\n| A1–A6, T1 | Existing delivery-state rules remain for act/tail; skipped for run. | Existing diagnostics |\n| P1 | `:wait` requires pending; `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`, `:ready` require pending absent. Run is admitted only in `:running`; `:ready` admits only act/tail. | `:pending-presence` |\n| E1, E2, K1–K3, X1 | Existing environment, continuation and task-context checks remain. | Existing diagnostics |\n| Q1 | A ready completion’s tag must be compatible with the delivery site, using the same site/reason relation as waits. | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| Q2 | Link/install completion `try?` is boolean; completed install does not require membership in the task’s installs. | `:pending-try` |\n| Q3 | Ready status is nil or a keyword; `:link-refused` requires link/install completion, `try? false`, and refusal data; other outcomes have no refusal slot. | `:ready-outcome` |\n| Q4 | Every update key names a cell in this task’s slot-9 census; update rows have unique keys and valid bodies. | Existing missing-cell/duplicate-key diagnostics |\n| Q5 | Every present completed operation ID passes shape-only validation. | `:malformed-op-id`, `:inexact` |\n\nQ1 and Q2 receive the completion descriptor through the validation context; they do not fabricate a pending field on the delivery frame. Ready FFI descriptors contribute their call IDs to task context alongside outstanding FFI waits, so parked FFI records remain valid while their response or sent-request outcome is queued.\n\n**Custody by kind.**\n\n| Site | Required behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `task-ops`, `custody-baseline`, `check-root-custody!` | Admit `:running` beside the existing kinds. |\n| `header-custody` | Running exclusive roots take the existing four-key arm, plus origin when present. |\n| `check-pendings!` | Validate task cardinalities, outstanding waits, installs, and completed descriptors through separate paths. |\n| `check-pendings!`, slot 18 | Invoke a factored `check-op-id-shape!` for every present completed operation ID. |\n| Existing `check-op-id!` | Call that shape helper, then retain origin, occurrence and counter checks for retained writes. |\n| `task-ops`, `protect!`, `with-custody` numbering | Continue to operate on outstanding writes; completed outcomes are excluded. |\n| `check-custody!` | Preserve the fenced/halted grant exemption. |\n| `check-install!` | Admit blocked child/parked phase and running child/running phase. |\n| `lower-task` | Preserve exclusive grant/protection requirements for non-fenced running roots. |\n\n`check-op-id-shape!` checks the existing two-field operation-ID shape, valid occurrence and exact sequence representation. It does not require root origin, compare the historical occurrence to the current root, or impose the current root’s sequence frontier. Nil remains the canonical absence of an ID for ungated operations.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-3; P2-5; glm N4/N5; terminal-status and FFI-context consequences of completed-state validation.\n\n### 3.2 Lift, lower, restore\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.2 completely.**\n\n**Lift, `lift-task*`:**\n\n1. Refuse the existing holds through `unliftable-hold`. Remove the ready-queue refusal.\n2. Classify kind by §3.1, preserving running → parked → blocked → halted precedence.\n3. Build the run from control, environment, K and `select-keys` of the current window over `L(pc)`; register its code/environment context for discharge.\n4. Build outstanding waits through the existing pending path.\n5. Build each ready entry through a separate completion path: delivery-state node, completion descriptor, lifted outcome value, optional status, explicit resource-update row and optional lifted refusal.\n6. Validate run frames in `:running`, ready delivery frames in `:ready`, and their completion descriptors through Q1–Q5. Outstanding waits remain in `:wait`.\n7. Preserve the existing store, parked-table, module-store, dependency discharge, module-pin and child-admission behavior.\n\n**Cursor identity and update-body encoding.**\n\nSlot 9 is the task’s sole cursor-cell census. `cell-for!` registers each referenced resource identity with its **current machine resource body**. On the shared-cursor sweep path, that body is already the final post-sweep body. Do not describe it as a pre-sweep position.\n\nFor each ready resource update:\n\n- Register or resolve the same resource identity through the task census.\n- Emit the update’s own `:cursor-cell-body` node directly from its stream and position.\n- Pair that node with the same canonical C in the ready update row.\n- Do not register the update body through a second `cell!` call.\n\nThus one C may be paired with different bodies across slot 9 and ready rows, or with equal bodies that Merkle sharing coalesces. Neither case creates a second cell identity.\n\nThe codec must distinguish **the census at task slot 9** from **update rows referenced by ready entries**. Existing blanket skips of every `:cursor-cells` row are not sufficient. Audit cell discovery, canonical numbering, emission and decoded validation: skip the census as a source of ordinary identity occurrences, but traverse ready update keys and bodies as structural references. Numbering uses the task census’s identities; update rows never establish a separate census. Missing census membership and duplicate keys refuse before attachment.\n\n**Lower/restore.**\n\nA running task restores:\n\n```clojure\n{:control {:segment local :pc pc}\n :window W\n :env E\n :k (when (seq K) K)\n :halted? false\n :blocked? false\n :value nil\n :wait-set entries}\n```\n\nEvery kind restores slot 18 in order. Resolve the ready state and completion descriptor, reconstruct the engine entry described in §3.1, then restore its value, status, refusal and resource updates. Update keys map through the slot-9 C→fresh-resource-key map; update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9.\n\nNo completed operation is polled or reissued by lower. A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition. Link/install completion restores without resurrecting retired pending resources.\n\nA fenced restore retains ready entries in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`, alongside recovery waits and parked records, and exposes an empty scheduler queue. Running control remains available for byte-identical relift, protected by the 6d-a closed-gate guard.\n\n**Backend refusal traversal.**\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
