{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '211,455p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n`lower-defect` inspects the slot-17 run through `state-prim`. For each slot-18 row it explicitly resolves the first cref to its delivery-state row, then calls `state-prim` on that row. It also calls `value-prim` on the outcome `:V` and optional refusal value. Any value-bearing completion fields that will be restored as backend payload receive the same traversal.\n\nThis replaces v2’s double-dereference expression. With the corrected grammar there is no intermediate wait row to dereference.\n\nThe independent test oracle traverses the same semantic roots independently of implementation helpers. Existing image-collision, free-environment, install and retained-FFI backend limits remain. Unsupported ready values must refuse before attachment.\n\n**Export classification.**\n\n`export/kind-of` follows the complete §3.1 precedence, including ready-only blocked tasks. Recovery metadata and validity checks include the ordered ready list. Export/abort/reopen preserve the list rather than executing it.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-4 adopts glm N1’s independent update-body encoding and muse N2’s shared-cursor interpretation; P2-7 and glm N2 fix ready-value traversal and cref resolution.\n\n### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Replace outcome 2, outcome 4 and the tests paragraph; retain outcomes 1 and 3 and explicit hand-off policy.**\n\n**2. Tenure dies after a completed slice.**\n\n`run-cycle` becomes a sequence of stage-local transitions. A stage receives the latest holder state and returns either a completed successor state or an explicit failure outcome containing the latest completed state.\n\n```text\nstage-result =\n  {:status :ok,     :state completed-state}\n  or\n  {:status :failed, :state latest-completed-state, :failure failure}\n```\n\nEach stage:\n\n1. Checks tenure against its input state.\n2. Executes its operation, retaining the existing tenure checks at external seams.\n3. Associates a successfully returned machine and stage metadata into a successor state.\n4. Passes that successor to the next stage.\n\nThe stage-local catch closes over that stage’s input state. A later tenure failure therefore returns the state containing the previous stage’s completed machine. It does not fall back to `step-active`’s original argument.\n\nThe failure consumer calls `end-run` with the state carried in the failure outcome. `end-run` retains its machine, gates it `:ended`, and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary` diagnostics. Journal-uncertain and inbox-refusal exceptions retain their existing propagation rules; they are not converted into ordinary program failures.\n\nIf an operation throws before returning a machine, retain the last completed stage, not an invented partial result. External effects already performed are not rolled back. No retained boundary receives new execution or publication authority.\n\n**4. Reopen and fencing.**\n\nA fenced running restore keeps control, window, environment and K, with gate `:exporting`. The kernel entry guard is already present from **6d-a**. It refuses `:exporting` and `:ended` before executing an instruction or entering a scheduler/bridge path.\n\nDirect `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` are covered. The guard remains outside the instruction loop. `rehydrate-fenced` must reproduce the published pack byte for byte, including ordered completed outcomes and cursor updates.\n\n**Tests, 6d-b.**\n\nRetain the five clock-controlled cuts from v2: before the slice, immediately after it, during replay/emit/read, between slices, and during running-root export. At each cut assert the **exact retained machine**, not merely its PC or the presence of diagnostics. A failure inside a stage retains its input machine; a failure after a completed stage retains its output machine.\n\nKeep the source fenced or ended, forbid effects after tenure loss, and verify either an authorized successor or recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain competing-candidate, uncertain-abort, expired-grant and recovery-byte-equality cases. Repeat the 6d-a execution-entry fencing checks through the holder’s reopened object.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-8 supplies an explicit exception-state channel; P1-2 moves fencing into 6d-a while retaining lifecycle tests in 6d-b.\n\n### 3.4 Fuel and budget propagation\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.4 completely.**\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, with positive exact integer `n`.\n\nOne invocation performs:\n\n1. Closed-gate check.\n2. Exactly one raw budgeted action:\n   - If control is present, `vm-hot` with fuel `n`.\n   - Otherwise, one budgeted scheduler round.\n3. If the result is blocked and an explicit bridge is installed, at most one `ffi/bridge-step`.\n4. Return that resulting machine immediately.\n\nThe implementation does **not** wrap the raw action in unchanged `ffi/maybe-run`. A handled response or malformed-request diagnostic does not restart the runner or bridge inside the same invocation. The next caller invocation resumes progress.\n\nThe bounded bridge helper may be private to the semantic runner or a bounded variant beside `ffi/maybe-run`; its contract is the four steps above. Existing unrestricted `vm/run` bridge behavior remains unchanged for live machines.\n\n**Budgeted scheduler round.**\n\nPropagate the budget through `check-wait-set`, `poll-links`, `advance-installs` and `advance-install`. Each visited child is invoked once through `run-fuel child n`; nested children use the same rule. Preserve existing gate stamping and the prohibition on running children under closed gates.\n\nAfter the sweep, resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation. A running child retains phase `:running`; blocked children retain `:parked`; halted children complete installation as today.\n\nThe dispatch tests **control presence**, not merely `engine/active-continuation?`: the latter only tests blocked/halted flags and can classify a control-less ingress-ready machine as active. Therefore no `run-fuel` path falls through `vm-hot` into its unbudgeted scheduler fallback. A quiescent control-less machine takes the budgeted no-work round and returns.\n\n**Per-tree bound.**\n\nEach task visited during the invocation receives at most one budgeted action and at most one bridge step. Each task executes at most `n` guest instructions. For `t` distinct tasks visited during the invocation, including children created during the round, the instruction bound is `n × t`. No task is revisited with a fresh budget inside that invocation.\n\n**The honest guarantee**, copied verbatim into the documents:\n\n> Fuel counts completed guest instructions. One `run-fuel` invocation executes at most `n` instructions per visited task, at most one scheduler round per visited inactive task, and at most one bridge step per visited task. A bridge result never restarts execution inside that invocation. No wall-clock bound is claimed for an instruction, host function, stream constructor, variable resolution, bridge step or scheduler sweep. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness and the composition chooses `n`.\n\n`vm/step` keeps its telemetry behavior. `run-fuel` adds no per-fuel snapshot; existing park, resume and bridge telemetry remains.\n\nRunning install children use the same carrier and receiver-admission checks as other tasks. The backends continue to refuse install children.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-1 bounds bridge execution; glm’s inactive-predicate omission is closed explicitly.\n\n### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow PCs; the register side\n\n**Replace v2’s displayed ordered equation and its explanatory paragraph. Retain jump/terminator derivation, stutter normalization, dead-prefix treatment and register allocation requirements.**\n\nLet:\n\n```text\nregister-sequence(p) = layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)\nexpected-values(p)   = [W[r] for r in register-sequence(p)]\n```\n\nAfter the separately specified dead-prefix treatment, compare the kernel region to `expected-values(p)` in emitter order. Its length equals the length of `register-sequence(p)`. Repeated occurrences of a virtual register occupy repeated positions and must decode to agreeing values.\n\nSeparately check:\n\n```text\nset(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p)) = L(p)   at reachable non-tail PCs\nset(uses(p))                 = L(p)   at reachable tail-call PCs\n```\n\nThere is no requirement that the length of the ordered sequence equal `|L(p)|`. Static checks validate layout availability and register sets; dynamic checks validate actual region length, order and repeated-register agreement.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-6 removes the false cardinality equation without weakening ordered reconstruction.\n\n### 3.6 Harness order, worlds, oracle, coverage\n\n**Replace the coverage and refusal-oracle paragraphs; retain v2’s lift → lower → immediate relift → law 6 → execution order and isolated worlds.**\n\n**Coverage.**\n\nThe stepping driver uses returned `run-fuel 1` boundaries. Its coverage classes are body entry, taken and untaken branch, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture, resume, park, FFI park, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, and running install child.\n\nBlocked-plus-ready is tested through an explicit **post-sweep/pre-resume fixture**, constructed with `check-wait-set`. It is not a mandatory returned boundary of the stepping driver. The shared-cursor fixture uses this same seam.\n\nReport driver coverage separately from constructed scheduler-product coverage. Pin per-case/per-host counts after measurement. Neither coverage inventory claims a theorem over all programs.\n\n**Refusal oracle.**\n\nThe independent test traversal covers:\n\n- Run windows and lexical bindings.\n- Every K-frame window and lexical binding.\n- Outstanding wait delivery states.\n- Ready delivery states and ready outcome values.\n- Optional ready refusal values and restored value-bearing completion fields.\n- Parked states and recursively reached collections, closures and continuations.\n\nFor register targets, exactly the expected unsupported points refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before attachment. Every other point in the admitted backend fragment transfers and continues to the reference outcome. Include a case whose only unsupported primitive is nested in a queued outcome.\n\nTerminal outcomes are compared as outcomes: receiver and source must raise the same classified failure with the same portable diagnostic data after identity normalization, rather than both being required to return a guest value.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-7; muse N4/glm O3; glm’s terminal-status acceptance omission.\n\n### 3.7 Amended document text\n\nThe v2 amendment marker and unaffected replacements remain. The following are complete replacements for the affected clauses.\n\n#### 3.7.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed §2.3 ready-row entry and associated note:**\n\n> A ready row contains a delivery-state reference, completed-operation descriptor, outcome value, optional status, cursor-update row and optional raised-refusal value. Its delivery state is act or tail, never run. It is judged in context `:ready`, without an outstanding pending.\n>\n> Completed descriptors cover ordinary reads and writes, FFI response and request-append outcomes, link-request and link-response outcomes, and install outcomes. Link/install completion does not require the request transport or install entry that existed before completion. A successful link/install outcome may have no status. Try failures return their carried value; ordinary require failures restore `:link-refused` and refusal data for the terminal-resume path.\n>\n> Slot 9 is the sole cell census. A ready update pairs an existing canonical C with its independently emitted cell body. One C may name different bodies across the census and ordered update rows. Restore maps C through the census’s fresh-key map and applies the update body carried by that ready row.\n\n**Replace the proposed §3.3 frame-context sentence:**\n\n> Validate every task context first; then judge outstanding waits in `:wait`, active run states in `:running`, ready delivery states in `:ready`, parked states in `:parked`, and reified states in `:captured`. Ready descriptors receive completed-operation validation separately from pending validation. FFI call IDs are collected from outstanding waits and completed outcomes.\n\n**Replace the proposed §5.6 item 5:**\n\n> Check ordered region length and contents against `layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)`, with repeated-register agreement and the specified dead-prefix treatment. Check the non-tail and tail set equations separately. Ordered multiplicity is not the cardinality of `L(p)`. Retain the parked-window check.\n\n**Replace proposed §7.1 step 1:**\n\n> **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observe wait, held entry, unminted cursor, pending close or link request without its cursor. These are driver-owned incomplete transitions. Their completion can depend on external acknowledgement or backpressure; there is no one-cycle resolution guarantee. There is no ready-queue quiescence refusal: completed outcomes travel in order.\n\n**Replace proposed §7.1’s ready-walk clause and §7.2’s ready-restore clause:**\n\n> Each ready entry contributes its delivery state, completed descriptor, lifted outcome and optional refusal, and explicit resource-update bodies. Completed descriptors do not re-enter outstanding-pending validation.\n\n> Restore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n\n**Replace the proposed §7.3 retention clause:**\n\n> Stage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\n**Replace the proposed §7.4 fuel text:** use §3.4 above, including its honest guarantee verbatim.\n\n**Replace the proposed §5.7 driver/oracle text:** use §3.6 above, including the separate post-sweep fixture and ready outcome roots.\n\n**Change:** Propagates consensus P1-1/2/3, P2-4/7/8, ordered checks, coverage hygiene and amended hold wording into normative design text.\n\n#### 3.7.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed §4.2 ready-row addition:**\n\n> The running row is `:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`. A completed outcome is `:ready [state-cref completion-cref value optional-status updates-cref optional-refusal]`; its act/tail delivery state has no outstanding pending. Completion descriptors and cursor updates follow the continuation design’s row grammar.\n\n**Append to the proposed §4.3 portability sentence:**\n\n> Receiver refusal checks include values waiting in completed outcomes, including nested values that become live only on resume.\n\n**Change:** Synchronizes the completed grammar and P2-7 oracle roots.\n\n#### 3.7.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Replace §7.7.4 step 1, located by “Quiesce: the ready-queue must be empty”:**\n\n> 1. **Retain the completed outcomes.** Enter the exporting gate and move the ordered ready queue into the retained export record. The live scheduler queue is empty. Preserve every outcome value, status, completion descriptor, cursor update and raised-refusal value. No queued operation is executed, discarded or reissued by this transition.\n\n**Replace v2’s proposed step-3 guard clause:**\n\n> The semantic execution entries refuse an exporting or ended machine before instruction, scheduler or bridge execution. This guard ships with running fenced restoration in 6d-a and remains outside the instruction loop.\n\n**Replace v2’s proposed “resolve in its next cycle” sentence:**\n\n> A task holding one of these incomplete driver transitions is not export-ready. Resolution may require repeated driver cycles, external acknowledgement or relief from backpressure; no fixed cycle count is promised.\n\n**Replace the proposed retained-boundary sentence:**\n\n> A holder whose tenure ends receives the latest completed state through stage-local failure handling, retains it for inspection, and offers nothing. Recovery remains the last durable checkpoint.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-9 closes the omitted Quiesce step; P1-2 and P2-8 synchronize fencing and retention; amended default 8 removes the false resolution promise.\n\n#### 3.7.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed ready-row, validation and completed-write clauses:**\n\n> The row grammar includes run, ready and completed-operation rows as defined in the continuation design. Ready delivery frames are validated in context `:ready`; completion descriptors are validated independently of outstanding pendings.\n>\n> The task’s slot-9 row owns the cell census. Ready update rows refer to that census and carry their own cell bodies. Canonical traversal distinguishes census ownership from update references; one C may pair with different bodies across rows.\n>\n> Completed operation IDs, when present, receive shape-only validation in the slot-18 path of `check-pendings!`. Retained-write origin, occurrence and frontier checks remain in `check-op-id!`. Completed outcomes are excluded from retained operations, numbering and `protect!`; no completed operation is reissued by lower or resume.\n>\n> The canonical round-trip law still requires no execution between lower and relift.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-3 and P2-4/5; corrected context inventory.\n\n#### 3.7.5–3.7.6\n\nRetain v2’s replacements. References to completed wakes mean the grammar of v2.1 §3.1; no new pending-resource requirement is implied.\n\n#### 3.7.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed I-9 entry:**\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
