{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '456,655p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"> **I-9 — 2026-10-16, any-PC.** The current lift rejects active control and queued completed outcomes, while the holder runs unrestricted computation. Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the carrier to run states and complete scheduler products. 6d-a includes completed stream/FFI/link/install outcomes, cursor-update encoding, bounded bridge-aware fuel and closed-gate execution guards. 6d-b adds explicit state retention on tenure failure and holder migration from fuel boundaries. 6e extends backend layouts and transfer laws. Earlier static-safepoint-only lift prescriptions are historical. No compatibility promise is added.\n\n**Change:** Makes the revision entry describe the reconciled scope and sequencing.\n\n#### 3.7.8 Assessment and known gaps\n\nRetain v2’s supersession of the assessment. Locate implementation defects by symbol and quoted text rather than obsolete offsets. The gap closes only when its corresponding acceptance gates pass; writing v2.1 does not mark implementation complete.\n\n**Change:** Citation hygiene and accurate implementation status.\n\n### 3.8 Inventory and citation policy\n\n**Replace v2’s line-offset policy and affected inventory rows.**\n\nThe source of truth is post-module-pins master. V2’s line numbers are historical locators; the “about +48” offset rule is withdrawn. For all retained inventory entries, use the named section/symbol and quoted text as the edit locator. Re-run the inventory on the implementation branch using:\n\n```text\nrg -n 'safepoint|not-at-safepoint|not-quiescent|Quiesce|quiescent' docs src test\n```\n\nHistorical logs remain append-only. Guest-event delivery safepoints remain a separate concept.\n\n| File / symbol or quoted anchor | Slice and disposition |\n|---|---|\n| `semantic.cljc` / `vm-hot`, IVM `step`, IVM `run` | 6d-a: closed-gate coverage before execution; guard outside instruction loop. |\n| `semantic.cljc` / proposed `run-fuel` | 6d-a: bounded action plus at most one bridge step; control-presence dispatch. |\n| `ffi.cljc` / `maybe-run`, “recur (run-fn vm)” | Existing unrestricted behavior retained; fuelled execution must not use this loop. |\n| `engine.cljc` / `wake-installed`, `poll-links`, `ready`, `refused-entry` | 6d-a: completed-outcome grammar covers their actual output. |\n| `rows.cljc` / `cell!`, `walk-cells`, `enter-node!`, decoded cell validation | 6d-a: one census identity; independently emitted update bodies; distinguish census from update rows. |\n| `rows.cljc` / `check-op-id!`, `check-pendings!` | 6d-a: factor shape validation; named slot-18 caller. |\n| `frame.cljc` / C3’s `:pc-body` | Keep diagnostic spelling; extend contexts with `:running` and `:ready`. |\n| `layout.cljc` / `lower-defect` | Resolve ready delivery state explicitly; inspect ready values and restored completion payloads. |\n| `driver.cljc` / `run-cycle`, `step-active` | 6d-b: stage-local failure outcomes carry the retained state. |\n| `reader.cljc` / `send-held` | Document backpressure and repeated cycles; no resolution deadline. |\n| UCF §7.7.4 / “Quiesce: the ready-queue must be empty” | 6d-b: replace with queue retention in the export record. |\n| Tuples / “Kind rules are” and “Canonical round trip” | Locate by quoted text; retain v2.1 kind rules and no-intervening-execution law. |\n\nBefore implementation sign-off, verify that no normative clause still assigns the guard to 6d-b, forbids completed link/install wakes, validates completed installs against live installs, restarts fuel through `maybe-run`, or promises one-cycle hold resolution.\n\n**Change:** muse O1/glm N3 citation hygiene; consensus P2-9; all affected code inventory entries synchronized.\n\n### 3.9 Slices, order, parallelism, effort\n\n**Replace the sequencing qualification and the 6d-a change/acceptance lists.**\n\nModule-pins commit 2 is present. Branch 6d-a from the integrated master. Remint affected fixtures once for this grammar revision.\n\n6d-a is **not described as fork-only**: it introduces the running carrier and fenced administrative lowering, and therefore ships the closed-gate guard. Holder-driven scheduling, publication and successor lifecycle remain 6d-b. After 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e may proceed independently, with 6d-b merging first if both are green.\n\n#### 6d-a: the reference lane\n\n**Change.**\n\n- Extend frame validation with run and ready contexts, correct C3 diagnostic spelling, and separate completion validation.\n- Add run, ready and completion rows; task slots 17/18; complete lift/lower/restore paths for every existing queue producer.\n- Implement update-body encoding and role-aware census traversal.\n- Factor operation-ID shape validation and invoke it from slot-18 completed validation.\n- Extend backend pre-attachment refusal traversal to ready values.\n- Add jump/terminator layouts and corrected static gates.\n- Propagate fuel through scheduler/install execution; use bounded bridge dispatch.\n- Add the closed-gate guard and cover all semantic execution entries.\n- Update the affected design text and fixtures with these behaviors.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n\n1. **Frame rules.** Run states at worked-example boundaries validate, including body start. Pin PC kind/bounds, window scope, missing/extra keys, forbidden deliver/pending and wrong-context diagnostics. C3 failures on act/return frames are `:pc-body`. Delivery frames use `saved(pc, rd)`; running frames use `L(pc)`.\n\n2. **Row grammar and completed validation.** Round-trip run frames; reject extra slots and incorrect reference kinds. Ready states admit act/tail only. Status is nil or keyword. Pin invalid completion tags, site mismatch, nonboolean link/install `try?`, inconsistent refusal slots, missing update-cell membership, duplicate update keys and malformed completed operation IDs. These lower refusals occur before attachment.\n\n3. **Task-product kinds.** Retain running-only, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, blocked-plus-ready and halted-plus-ready cases. Add halted-plus-outstanding-wait with empty queue: pin the existing blocked normalization and dropped result separately. Retain invalid run counts, illegal result/parked-id and empty-blocked cases.\n\n4. **Completed link/install outcomes.** Exercise success, try failure and ordinary raised failure for link/install producers. Capture completed install outcomes after removal of the install entry. Include active control beside a remaining completion. Immediate relift is equal; continuation delivers or raises once, without reconstructing retired pending resources.\n\n5. **Shared cursor.** Two waiters share one cursor. Snapshot after the sweep before any resume. Preserve one canonical cell identity, the slot-9 current body, both values and update rows in order. Assert immediate relift equality and source/receiver continuation equality. Add an encoding case where one C pairs with different bodies across census/update rows.\n\n6. **Completed write and terminal outcomes.** Fork and exclusive completed-write histories round-trip without enrollment or reappend. A malformed present ID refuses at the named slot-18 check. Nil IDs remain admissible where the ungated source produced none. Add a terminal-status ready case whose receiver raises identically, plus the FFI sent-request transition that creates a response wait without a second request append.\n\n7. **Every returned boundary, slow tier.** Run the existing corpus through `run-fuel 1`; lift, lower into isolated worlds, immediately relift, validate frames and continue to the reference outcome. Pin measured counts and the driver coverage classes of §3.6. Constructed post-sweep cases are reported separately.\n\n8. **Quick sample and products.** Retain v2’s structural sample and every-boundary small rows. Run the scheduler products and shared-cursor seam through the harness. Retain cursor-ref, reified-continuation, nil-live-register, `ghost` and hold-refusal cases.\n\n9. **Layout gates.** At every reachable corpus PC, check register injectivity, jump/terminator layout availability and separate non-tail/tail set equations. On executed paths compare ordered region length and values to the ordered register sequence, including repeated-register agreement. Do not compare that sequence length to `|L(pc)|`.\n\n10. **Budgeted children.** Preempt long and nested initializers; lift/lower running children under receiver admission; continue to linked. Count actual instruction transitions and assert at most `n` per visited task and `n × t` overall. No child receives a second budget inside one invocation.\n\n11. **Bounded bridge execution.** A program repeatedly making bridge-served FFI calls returns from every `run-fuel` invocation. Each invocation executes at most one raw action and one bridge step. Repeated invocations reach the reference result. Diagnostic-only bridge input also returns after one step. Pin control-less ingress-ready dispatch to the budgeted round. For a pure active computation lasting at least `n` instructions, compare with `n` single-instruction steps; an earlier park/halt ends the comparison there.\n\n12. **Fencing.** Lower a running snapshot with `{:fenced true}` and exercise direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel`. Each refuses the closed gate before guest, scheduler or bridge work. Assert unchanged control/window/environment/K, unchanged ready recovery data and unchanged relift bytes. Cover `:ended` as well as `:exporting`.\n\n13. **Ready-value portability.** The only unsupported primitive is nested in the ready outcome; register-target lower refuses before attachment. Retain the corresponding portable positive case. Test ready delivery state, outcome and restored descriptor roots independently.\n\n14. **Landing checks.** Kondo clean; the quick tier passes on JVM, Node and Dart with every touched namespace present in logs; the slow tier runs once at landing on all three hosts. Remint fixtures from the integrated grammar. Review stops on satisfied gates, not a fixed round count.\n\n**Change:** All three P1s and all reconciled P2/P3 acceptance amendments are now explicit 6d-a gates.\n\n#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n**Replace v2’s numbered change list.**\n\n1. Add positive-integer-or-nil holder `:fuel`; use `run-fuel` when configured.\n2. Implement §3.3’s stage-local state-bearing failure channel and exact retained-machine diagnostics.\n3. Update export classification, queue retention, recovery metadata, abort/reinstatement and fenced rehydration for completed outcomes.\n4. Extend ledger/grant offerable kinds with `:running`.\n5. Retain the execution-entry guard from 6d-a; add holder integration tests rather than introducing the guard here.\n6. Keep v2’s custody race, grant, expiry, competing-candidate and Stage E tests; add exact retained-machine equality at every cut.\n7. For each hold class, test whether normal conditions resolve it in one cycle. Record any failure of that property in known gaps, including a backpressure fixture that deliberately persists across cycles. Do not make one-cycle resolution a runtime promise.\n8. Apply the 6d-b document amendments, including replacement of UCF’s Quiesce step.\n\n**Change:** P1-2 sequencing, P2-8 state transport, P2-9 documentation and amended default 8.\n\n#### 6e: the cross-engine half\n\nRetain v2’s scope and acceptance, replacing references to its oracle and cardinality equation with §§3.5–3.6 above. Ready handling uses the completed-outcome grammar, not pending-wait reconstruction. The backend fragment and existing obstruction list remain unchanged.\n\n**Change:** Propagates ready-value and ordered-layout corrections into cross-engine gates.\n\n#### Effort and routing\n\nThe v2 estimates remain planning estimates: 4.5 days for 6d-a, 4 for 6d-b, 5 for 6e, plus the stated documentation allocation. The added gates are not optional to preserve those estimates.\n\nThis v2.1 draft is by codex; glm confirms it. Fable diff-confirms Tuesday 2026-10-20 before any merge, as commissioned. Implementation assignment remains the orchestrator’s responsibility. No merge or implementation completion is asserted by this document.\n\n**Change:** Aligns routing with the owner’s current commission and preserves gate-based review.\n\n### 3.10 Decisions for the owner — ratified defaults\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.10 completely.**\n\n1. **Task slots 17 (`run`) and 18 (`ready`).** Keep separate typed slots and remint affected roots after module-pins commit 2. This removes mixed-slot positional misreads.\n\n2. **Holder `:fuel`, default nil.** Nil retains current unrestricted behavior; compositions opt into instruction-budget preemption explicitly.\n\n3. **No automatic export at the bound in 6d-b.** `hand-off` remains explicit and `:yin.k/at-boundary` supplies the signal. A later tenure-aware automatic policy belongs in composition.\n\n4. **No adoption of a retained boundary.** Ended holders retain inspection data only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. A later same-holder regrant/adoption rule would be new ledger authority.\n\n5. **Keep Q6 edge-reachability liveness.** Pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal. Constant propagation remains a separate slice.\n\n6. **Completed `:put` wakes are completed outcomes, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`, and never re-appended.** Separate operation-ID shape validation from retained-write custody validation. Factor `check-op-id-shape!` from `check-op-id!`; call it from `check-pendings!` over slot 18 for every present completed operation ID. The retained-write path additionally keeps its origin, occurrence and sequence-frontier checks. Completed fork history requires no root custody context. Pin malformed-ID refusal and valid fork/exclusive completed histories.\n\n7. **Relabel stack `:push` to its successor.** No guest semantics run; the value is already on the stack.\n\n8. **Holds remain refusals.** Observe entries, held observations, unminted cursors, pending closes and link requests without installed cursors remain incomplete driver transitions. Backpressure or acknowledgement delay may preserve them across arbitrarily many cycles. The documents promise no one-cycle resolution. 6d-b still tests each hold’s one-cycle behavior under stated fixture conditions and records any nonresolving case in known gaps; that reporting duty does not convert external progress into a guarantee.\n\n**Change:** Writes the owner-ratified wording directly into defaults 6 and 8; defaults 1–5 and 7 retain their policies.\n\n### 3.11 Risks\n\n**Replace v2 risk 2 and append the following qualifications.**\n\n2. **Queue producer completeness.** Every existing producer must have a completed descriptor and acceptance case in 6d-a. Discovering another existing producer is a defect to fix before that slice lands, not permission to ship `:ready-reason` refusal for supported execution.\n\n8. **Cursor traversal coupling.** Adding update rows requires auditing every census-special-case traversal. Passing ordinary next/put cases alone does not establish canonical numbering for shared identities.\n\n9. **External progress.** Fuel bounds guest instruction work, not host duration, acknowledgement latency or hold resolution. Expiry recovery remains checkpoint-based.\n\n10. **Stage failures.** Retention preserves the last completed stage. It does not claim transactional rollback or recovery of an operation’s unreturned intermediate locals.\n\n**Change:** Removes the link-wake deferral and records the reconciled limits without weakening acceptance.\n\n## 4. Verification honesty\n\n**Replaces v2 §4 completely for this amendment edition.**\n\nThe consensus, muse findings, glm findings and v2 were read completely in the requested order. New implementation citations below were checked against this directory’s tree. Proposed helpers and row kinds are specifications, not claims that those definitions already exist.\n\n| Verified site | Relevant fact |\n|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:278` and `:334` | `bridge-step` performs one bridge operation; `maybe-run` can repeat runner/bridge work. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:137`, `:271`, `:331` | Hot-loop fallback, scheduler wrapper and public protocol execution paths. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:258` | `active-continuation?` checks flags, not control presence. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1148` | Link completion removes envelope/request/response/cursor fields. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1156` | Try failures return data; ordinary failures retain status and refusal. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1327`, `:1627` | Install and link outcomes enter the ready queue. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:49` | Existing row descriptors support act/tail references and discriminated pending rows. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1243` | Operation-ID shape and custody checks currently share one function. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1388` | `cell!` is first-wins per source identity. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:397`, `:468`, `:819` | Existing traversal skips are keyed by `:cursor-cells` kind. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1131` | Task validation currently gathers FFI IDs from waits. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2242` | Restore reconstructs pending entries and FFI response transitions. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc:162` | Existing context, site/reason and try validation paths. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:2117`, `:2252` | Cycle locals and outer failure handling are separate scopes. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader.cljc:484` | Full append preserves the held observation for later cycles. |\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1904` | The omitted Quiesce requirement exists. |\n\nNo new code citation is knowingly unverified. Inherited v2 offsets are explicitly historical; symbol/quoted-text locators govern their application. Corpus counts, revised point counts, runtime bounds and cross-host behavior remain acceptance work, not executed evidence.\n\n## 5. Readiness\n\n6d-a is implementation-ready under the ratified defaults and the complete replacement text above. Module-pins commit 2 is already present. The bounded bridge path, execution-entry fencing and complete link/install outcome grammar are mandatory 6d-a work.\n\n6d-b and 6e depend on the resulting grammar. Holder-level closure is claimed only after 6d-b’s lifecycle gates pass. Fable’s required diff confirmation remains a pre-merge gate.\n\n**Residual risks**\n\n- **Halted plus outstanding waits:** the consensus explicitly retains today’s blocked normalization and dropped halt value. The source’s `vm/value` can nevertheless expose that value before further scheduling. V2’s blanket law-1 wording must not be treated as proof of equality for that immediate inspection. V2.1 pins the existing normalization separately; any expansion of the observation contract requires an explicit ruling.\n- **V2’s act/run acceptance wording:** its phrase “an act … and a run … with rd present both validate” is ambiguous and, if applied to the act window, conflicts with `saved(pc, rd)`. Acceptance item 1 now names the two window sets explicitly; reviewers should confirm that this is the intended reading.\n- **Static versus dynamic evidence:** actual stack-region contents cannot be established by a static layout-only test. V2.1 separates static set/layout checks from executed ordered-region checks. Neither is presented as a universal proof.\n\nPLAN-V2.1-READY\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
