{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*frame*test*' -g '*rows*test*'; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*frame*test*' -g '*rows*test*'; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"?? collab/1792146800000-engineer-6d-a.prompt.md\nAGENTS.md\ntest/dao/stream/ws/browser_test.cljs\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/fencing_rows_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_rows_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/compose_rows_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/frame_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n(ns yin.vm.semantic.frame\n  \"Pure, ordered validation of decoded canonical continuation frames (§3).\"\n  (:require [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.analysis :as analysis]))\n\n\n(defn- problem\n  [kind path]\n  {:yin.k/kind kind :yin.k/path path})\n\n\n(defn- closed?\n  [x required optional]\n  (and (map? x) (every? #(contains? x %) required)\n       (every? (into (set required) optional) (keys x))))\n\n\n(defn task-defect\n  \"X1, once per body, including a halted body with no frames.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [e (:free-env task)]\n    (cond\n      (not (and (map? e) (every? symbol? (keys e))))\n      (problem :free-env-shape [:free-env])\n      (some vm/reserved-name? (keys e))\n      (problem :reserved-name [:free-env]))))\n\n\n(defn- env?\n  [e]\n  (and (closed? e [:yin.k/bindings] [:yin.k/store-of])\n       (or (not (contains? e :yin.k/store-of)) (some? (:yin.k/store-of e)))\n       (map? (:yin.k/bindings e))\n       (every? symbol? (keys (:yin.k/bindings e)))))\n\n\n(defn- lexical-names\n  [v pc]\n  (let [bs (code/body-ranges v)]\n    (loop [b (code/body-index bs pc) names #{}]\n      (if-let [owner (:owner (nth bs b))]\n        (recur (code/body-index bs owner) (into names (nth (nth v owner) 2)))\n        names))))\n\n\n(defn- window-defect\n  [f v rd prefix path]\n  (let [pc (:yin.k/pc f) w (:yin.k/window f)\n        body (nth (code/parse v) (code/body-index (code/body-ranges v) pc))\n        wanted (analysis/saved (analysis/analyze v) pc rd)\n        ks (set (keys w))\n        kind (fn [s] (keyword (str prefix s)))\n        p (conj path :yin.k/window)]\n    (cond\n      (some #(>= % (:registers body)) ks) (problem (kind \"window-scope\") p)\n      (contains? w rd) (problem (kind \"window-self\") p)\n      (some #(not (contains? wanted %)) ks) (problem (kind \"window-extra\") p)\n      (some #(not (contains? w %)) wanted) (problem (kind \"window-missing\") p))))\n\n\n(defn- bindings=\n  \"Compare free bindings slotwise by encoder content, retaining host equality\n   for callables and stream, cursor or cell references as data-content= does.\"\n  [a b]\n  (let [opaque? (fn [x]\n                  (or (fn? x) (values/closure? x) (values/continuation? x)\n                      (and (map? x) (contains? #{:stream-ref :cursor-ref :cell-ref} (:type x)))))]\n    (and (= (set (keys a)) (set (keys b)))\n         (every? (fn [[k v]]\n                   (let [w (get b k)]\n                     (or (identical? v w)\n                         (if (or (opaque? v) (opaque? w))\n                           (= v w)\n                           (cbor/content= v w))))) a))))\n\n\n(defn defect\n  \"First §3 defect, or nil. Code is already well formed; task is plain\n   {:free-env map :parked-key P :ffi-call-ids set}. No engine is read.\n   Values have already passed the carrier's value-domain checks.\"\n  [frame code-map context task]\n  (let [act? (= :act (:yin.k/state frame))\n        required (if act?\n                   [:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env\n                    :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k]\n                   [:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k])\n        k (:yin.k/k frame)\n        returns (when (vector? k) (mapv (fn [i] [(nth k i) [:yin.k/k i]]) (range (count k))))\n        all (into [[frame []]] returns)\n        envs (if act? all returns)]\n    (or\n      ;; S1-S4, globally before any dependent read.\n      (when-not (and (contains? #{:act :tail} (:yin.k/state frame))\n                     (closed? frame required [:yin.k/pending]))\n        (problem :frame-shape []))\n      (when-not (vector? k) (problem :k-shape [:yin.k/k]))\n      (some (fn [[f p]]\n              (when-not (and (closed? f [:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc\n                                         :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd] [])\n                             (= :return (:yin.k/frame-type f)))\n                (problem :k-shape p))) returns)\n      (some (fn [[f p]]\n              (when-not (and (map? (:yin.k/window f))\n                             (every? code/reg? (keys (:yin.k/window f))))\n                (problem :window-representation (conj p :yin.k/window)))) envs)\n      (some (fn [[f p]]\n              (when-not (env? (:yin.k/env f))\n                (problem :env-shape (conj p :yin.k/env)))) envs)\n      (when-not (map? (:yin.k/deliver frame)) (problem :deliver-shape [:yin.k/deliver]))\n      ;; C1-C3 across the entire continuation.\n      (some (fn [[f p]]\n              (when-not (contains? code-map (:yin.k/segment f))\n                (problem :segment (conj p :yin.k/segment)))) all)\n      (some (fn [[f p]]\n              (let [slot (if (and (empty? p) (not act?)) :yin.k/site :yin.k/pc)\n                    n (get f slot) v (get code-map (:yin.k/segment f))]\n                (cond\n                  (not (code/reg? n)) (problem :pc-kind (conj p slot))\n                  (not (and (< n (count v)) (or (= slot :yin.k/site) (pos? n))))\n                  (problem :pc-bounds (conj p slot))))) all)\n      (some (fn [[f p]]\n              (let [pc (:yin.k/pc f) bs (code/body-ranges (get code-map (:yin.k/segment f)))]\n                (when-not (= (code/body-index bs (dec pc)) (code/body-index bs pc))\n                  (problem :pc-body (conj p :yin.k/pc))))) envs)\n      (let [v (get code-map (:yin.k/segment frame))\n            site (if act? (dec (:yin.k/pc frame)) (:yin.k/site frame))\n            inst (nth v site) op (first inst) rd (code/rd inst)\n            d (:yin.k/deliver frame)]\n        (or\n          (if act?\n            (or\n              (when-not (or (contains? #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation} op)\n                            (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? inst))))\n                (problem :site [:yin.k/pc]))\n              (when-not (= rd (:yin.k/rd d)) (problem :destination [:yin.k/deliver :yin.k/rd]))\n              (when-not (= {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd rd} d) (problem :deliver [:yin.k/deliver]))\n              (window-defect frame v rd \"\" []))\n            (or\n              (when-not (code/tail-call? inst) (problem :site [:yin.k/site]))\n              (when-not (= {:yin.k/deliver :return} d) (problem :deliver [:yin.k/deliver]))))\n          (some (fn [[f p]]\n                  (let [fv (get code-map (:yin.k/segment f)) fi (nth fv (dec (:yin.k/pc f)))\n                        frd (:yin.k/rd f)]\n                    (or\n                      (when-not (and (= :call (first fi)) (not (code/tail-call? fi)))\n                        (problem :return-site (conj p :yin.k/pc)))\n                      (when-not (= frd (code/rd fi)) (problem :return-destination (conj p :yin.k/rd)))\n                      (window-defect f fv frd \"return-\" p)))) returns)\n          (some (fn [[f p]]\n                  (when (some vm/reserved-name? (keys (get-in f [:yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings])))\n                    (problem :reserved-name (conj p :yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings)))) envs)\n          (some (fn [[f p]]\n                  (let [names (lexical-names (get code-map (:yin.k/segment f)) (:yin.k/pc f))\n                        bindings (get-in f [:yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings])]\n                    (when-not (and (every? #(contains? bindings %) names)\n                                   (bindings= (apply dissoc bindings names)\n                                              (apply dissoc (:free-env task) names)))\n                      (problem :free-env-mismatch (conj p :yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings))))) envs)\n          (when-not (and (contains? #{:wait :captured :parked} context)\n                         (= (= :wait context) (contains? frame :yin.k/pending)))\n            (problem :pending-presence [:yin.k/pending]))\n          (when (= :wait context)\n            (let [reasons (case op\n                            :stream-next #{:next}\n                            :stream-put #{:put}\n                            :ffi-call #{:ffi :ffi-request}\n                            :call #{:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install}\n                            #{})]\n              (when-not (contains? reasons (get-in frame [:yin.k/pending :yin.k/reason]))\n                (problem :reason-mismatch [:yin.k/pending :yin.k/reason]))))\n          (when (and (= :wait context)\n                     (contains? #{:link-request :link-response :install}\n                                (get-in frame [:yin.k/pending :yin.k/reason]))\n                     (not (boolean? (get-in frame [:yin.k/pending :yin.k/try?]))))\n            (problem :pending-try [:yin.k/pending :yin.k/try?]))\n          (when (and (= :captured context) (not (and act? (= :current-continuation op))))\n            (problem :captured-site [(if act? :yin.k/pc :yin.k/site)]))\n          (when (and (= :parked context)\n                     (not (or (and act? (= :park op))\n                              (and (contains? (:ffi-call-ids task) (:parked-key task))\n                                   (or (and act? (= :ffi-call op))\n                                       (and (not act?) (code/tail-call? inst)))))))\n            (problem :parked-site [(if act? :yin.k/pc :yin.k/site)])))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,170p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1,180p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc; sed -n '125,155p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '265,345p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '1450,1510p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; rg -n 'defn.*(advance-installs|poll-links|check-wait-set|scheduler-round)|advance-install|poll-links' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,170p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1,180p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc; sed -n '125,155p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '265,345p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '1450,1510p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; rg -n 'defn.*(advance-installs|poll-links|check-wait-set|scheduler-round)|advance-install|poll-links' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.vm.ucf.rows\n  \"Version-3 row codec. Pure row construction, graph decoding and\n   discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1), then full task\n   transport for the semantic kernel (continuation design §7):\n   lift-task walks a quiescent machine into an abstract task and emits it;\n   lower-task decodes, checks and restores one into a fresh receiver.\n   An abstract row is {:kind keyword :slots vector}. Structural references\n   contain abstract rows; values use [:lit d], [:row abstract-row], [:cursor-cell id].\n   A task's :cursor-cells row holds alternating source identities and :cursor-cell-body\n   rows. Install children are separate tasks with separate cursor-cell scopes.\n\n   The census is ordered numerically by n (draft §3, \\\"sorted by n\\\"), so\n   :yin.k/cc-10 follows :yin.k/cc-9; its keys must spell cc-N with no leading\n   zero, else :slot-kind.\n\n   Default value markers are plain maps {:yin.k/row abstract-row} and\n   {:yin.k/cursor-cell keyword}. A guest map whose sole key is :yin.k/row or\n   :yin.k/cursor-cell collides with them and is not portable: on encode and decode\n   every literal of that shape is refused :yin.k/non-portable. Explicit\n   markers use the caller value encoder.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.frame :as frame]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.scan :as scan]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.custody :as custody]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.remote :as remote]))\n\n\n(def profile {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1})\n\n\n(def pending-table\n  {:next [:C]\n   :put [[:ref :stream] :V :D]\n   :ffi [:C :D :D]\n   :ffi-request [:D :V [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :D]\n   :link-request [:D :S [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :D :D :D]\n   :link-response [:D :S [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :D]\n   :install [:S :D]})\n\n\n(def row-table\n  \"Slot descriptors, excluding id and kind. :repeat/:pairs are variadic;\n   :refs is an ordered vector of structural references. :pending is\n   discriminated by pending-table. Nothing in this table is executable.\"\n  {:vec [:repeat :V] :list [:repeat :V] :set [:repeat :V]\n   :map [:pairs :V :V] :bindings [:pairs :S :V] :store [:pairs :D :V]\n   :env [[:ref :bindings] :optional-D]\n   :closure [:A :N :syms [:ref :bindings] :optional-D]\n   :prim [:S] :stream [:D :D] :reified [[:ref :act]] :parked-ref [:D]\n   :act [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k] :N]\n   :tail [:A :N [:ref :k]]\n   :window [:pairs :N :V] :k [:repeat [:ref :kframe]]\n   :kframe [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] :N]\n   :wait [[:ref :act :tail] [:ref :pending]] :pending :pending\n   :module-store [:D [:ref :store]]\n   :parked-table [:pairs :D [:ref :act :tail]]\n   :install [:S :D :D :D [:ref :task]]\n   :cursor-cells [:pairs :C [:ref :cursor-cell-body]] :cursor-cell-body [[:ref :stream] :D]\n   :task [:N :D :D :D :N [:ref :bindings] [:ref :store]\n          [:refs :module-store] [:ref :parked-table] [:ref :cursor-cells]\n          :D :D [:refs :install] [:refs :wait] :optional-D :optional-V :optional-D]})\n\n\n(defn node\n  [kind slots]\n  {:kind kind :slots (if (= :window kind)\n                       (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n                       (vec slots))})\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  [status kind path]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Row codec refused data\"\n                  {:yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind :yin.k/path path})))\n\n\n(defn- bad!\n  [kind path]\n  (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable kind path))\n\n\n(defn- marker-shaped?\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x) (= 1 (count x)) (or (contains? x :yin.k/row) (contains? x :yin.k/cursor-cell))))\n\n\n(defn- check-literal-markers!\n  [x]\n  (when (marker-shaped? x) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :marker []))\n  (when (coll? x)\n    (doseq [v (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) x)] (check-literal-markers! v)))\n  (when (meta x) (check-literal-markers! (meta x))))\n\n\n(defn- bytes-key\n  [bs]\n  ;; Unsigned bytewise order, without a length-first comparison.\n  (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq bs)))\n\n\n(defn- compare-bytes\n  [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond\n      (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n      (= i (count b)) 1\n      (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n      :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n\n(defn- byte-compare\n  [a b]\n  (compare-bytes (bytes-key (cbor/encode a)) (bytes-key (cbor/encode b))))\n\n\n(defn- sorted-bytes\n  [xs]\n  (vec (sort byte-compare xs)))\n\n\n(defn- descriptors\n  [kind slots]\n  ;; :entry is the numbering's synthetic map entry [:entry k v], never a carrier row.\n  (let [schema (if (= :entry kind) [:V :V] (get row-table kind))]\n    (cond\n      (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get pending-table (first slots)))\n      (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n      (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n      :else schema)))\n\n\n(defn- ref?\n  [d]\n  (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n\n\n(defn- refs?\n  [d]\n  (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n\n\n(def ^:private census-key #\"cc-([1-9][0-9]*)\")\n\n\n(defn- census-order\n  ;; Numeric order of cc-N without reading wire data: fewer digits first,\n  ;; then the digits. Other keys (a partial emit-numbered) sort last.\n  [k]\n  (let [digits (when (keyword? k) (second (re-matches census-key (name k))))]\n    (if digits [0 (count digits) digits] [1 0 (str k)])))\n\n\n(defn- ordered-slots\n  [kind slots]\n  (cond\n    (= kind :window) (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n    (= kind :cursor-cells) (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by #(census-order (first %)) (partition 2 slots))))\n    (contains? #{:map :bindings :store :parked-table} kind)\n    (vec (mapcat identity (sort #(byte-compare (first %1) (first %2)) (partition 2 slots))))\n    (= kind :set) (sorted-bytes slots)\n    :else slots))\n\n(ns yin.vm.semantic.layout\n  \"Phase 6c (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md §4):\n   the de Bruijn register and stack kernels reconstruct the canonical\n   frame of the semantic kernel through their layouts, and lower it\n   back.\n\n   Static half, per segment A: `segment` derives R(A) and H(A) and reads\n   off the register pairing (`phys`, the restricted inverse `virt`, the\n   kernel's `live` as live-out with tail boundaries `[]`), the stack\n   correspondence (`h->a`, `a->h`, every `:push` removed) and the operand\n   layout `stack-layout` (the values pushed and not yet consumed while\n   control is inside a pc's expression). A `space` indexes segments by\n   the kernels' image checksums, R and H: A is the\n   only code identity.\n\n   Dynamic half: `lift-state` reads a kernel's wait entry, parked record\n   or reified payload as the canonical `act` or `tail` payload the\n   semantic evaluator mints (`{:segment A :pc :env :window :k\n   :deliver}`, `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, engine fields carried);\n   `lower-state` is its inverse. The semantic kernel's return\n   destinations are recovered from A, never from the physical `:dest`;\n   the stack kernel's operand stack is sliced `[previous-base, base)` per\n   frame and lowered with cumulative bases. `E` is the free environment\n   merged under the chain's positional frames (§4.4); the inverse reads\n   each frame back from `E` by the chain, never the free environment.\n\n   Task level: `canonical-task` presents a quiescent kernel as a\n   semantic machine for `yin.vm.ucf.rows/lift-task`;\n   `kernel-task` restores a machine `rows/lower-task` produced into a\n   fresh kernel; `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose them with the row\n   carrier, the lower refusing what a kernel cannot hold before any\n   stream is attached. A layout never runs code, never fills a binding\n   from the receiver and never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as register]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as stack]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  [status kind data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" (merge data (assoc {} :yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Static half: one segment\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- operands\n  \"The register operands of an A tuple, destination first, then its uses\n   in table order.\"\n  [t]\n  (into (if-some [r (code/rd t)] [r] []) (code/uses t)))\n\n\n(defn- register-operands\n  \"The `:reg`/`:regs` operands of an R tuple in table order (the `:live`\n   slot is `:data`, never paired).\"\n  [t]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [[[_ kind] x]] (case kind :reg [x] :regs x [])))\n        (map vector (get rcode/opcode-table (nth t 0)) (rest t))))\n\n\n(defn register-pairs\n  \"`[pc virtual physical]` for every register operand of every pc: A's\n   tuple and R's name their registers in the same slots (phase-5 §2.4).\"\n  [v image]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [pc]\n                  (map (fn [x s] [pc x s])\n                       (operands (nth v pc))\n                       (register-operands (nth (:instructions image) pc)))))\n        (range (count v))))\n\n\n(defn- phys-maps\n  \"Per body, `{virtual physical}` read off the pairs; the first pc that\n   names a virtual wins (register-pairing checks they agree).\"\n  [bodies pairs]\n  (reduce (fn [acc [pc x s]]\n            (let [b (code/body-index bodies pc)]\n              (if (contains? (nth acc b) x) acc (assoc-in acc [b x] s))))\n          (vec (repeat (count bodies) {}))\n          pairs))\n\n\n(defn correspondence\n  \"§4.3 static item 1: H with every `:push` removed corresponds one to one,\n   in order, with A. Answers `{:a->h [h-pc …] :h->a {h-pc a-pc}}`.\"\n  [h]\n  (reduce (fn [acc hpc]\n            (if (= :push (nth (nth h hpc) 0))\n              acc\n              (-> acc\n                  (update :a->h conj hpc)\n                  (assoc-in [:h->a hpc] (count (:a->h acc))))))\n          {:a->h [] :h->a {}}\n          (range (count h))))\n\n\n(def ^:private pushing-ops\n  \"The parse nodes whose children before the current one are pushed:\n   `:call` (the operator and earlier operands), `:ffi-call` (earlier\n   operands) and `:stream-put` (the target, while the value runs).\"\n  #{:call :ffi-call :stream-put})\n\n\n(defn- stack-layouts\n  \"§4.3 static item 2 for every pc of every body: `{pc [virtual …]}`,\n   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n  [v bodies]\n  (letfn [(walk\n            [out node pending]\n            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n                      out\n                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n\n\n(defn segment\n  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n        r (derive/register-image v)\n        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n    (merge {:vector v\n            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n            :bodies bodies\n            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n            :register r\n            :register-id (rcode/register-hash r)\n            :stack h\n            :stack-id (dcode/image-hash h)\n            :pairs pairs\n            :phys (phys-maps bodies pairs)\n            :layouts (stack-layouts v bodies)}\n           (correspondence h))))\n\n\n(defn space\n  \"Segments by A, and A by each kernel's image identity. `segments` is a\n   seq of `segment` results or of A vectors.\"\n  [segments]\n  (reduce (fn [acc s]\n            (let [s (if (map? s) s (segment s))\n                  a (:address s)]\n              (-> acc\n                  (assoc-in [:segments a] s)\n                  (update-in [:register (:register-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a)\n                  (update-in [:stack (:stack-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a))))\n          {:segments {} :register {} :stack {}}\n          segments))\n\n\n(defn segment-of\n  \"The segment A names in `space`.\"\n  [space a]\n  (or (get-in space [:segments a])\n      (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n\n\n(defn- image-segment\n  \"The segment whose `kind` image is `ident`: an identity is a checksum,\n   so two A deriving one image are ambiguous and refused.\"\n  [space kind ident]\n                          :value value))\n         (throw (ex-info \"Entry carries no delivery record\" {:rule :deliver :entry entry})))))))\n\n\n(defn- scheduler-round\n  [machine]\n  (engine/scheduler-round machine register-restore))\n\n\n(defn vm-hot\n  \"Execute fuel instructions with the activation in locals; nil fuel runs to exit.\"\n  [machine fuel]\n  (if (and (nil? (:control machine)) (nil? (:k machine)))\n    (scheduler-round machine)\n    (let [{:keys [segment pc]} (:control machine)]\n      (loop [machine machine seg segment pc pc W (:window machine)\n             E (:env machine) K (or (:k machine) []) fuel fuel]\n        (if (and fuel (zero? fuel))\n          (put-registers machine seg pc W E K)\n          (let [image (get (:code machine) seg)\n                inst (nth (:vector image) pc)\n                op (nth inst 0)\n                remaining (when fuel (dec fuel))]\n            (case op\n              :const (recur machine seg (inc pc) (assoc W (nth inst 1) (nth inst 2)) E K remaining)\n              :var (recur machine seg (inc pc)\n                          (assoc W (nth inst 1)\n                                 (engine/resolve-var E\n                                                     (engine/active-store machine (engine/env-store-of E))\n                                                     (:primitives machine) (:modules machine) (nth inst 2)))\n                          E K remaining)\n                        (if (:halted? restored) restored\n                            (recur restored (:segment control) (:pc control) (:window restored)\n                                   (:env restored) (or (:k restored) []) remaining)))\n              (throw (ex-info \"Unknown register instruction\" {:op op})))))))))\n\n\n(defn- register-run-scheduler\n  [machine]\n  (engine/run-loop machine engine/active-continuation? #(vm-hot % nil)\n                   #(engine/resume-from-run-queue % register-restore)))\n\n\n(defn- store-image\n  [images image]\n  (let [seg (:segment image)]\n    (if-let [existing (find images seg)]\n      (if (= (:address (val existing)) (:address image)) images\n          (throw (ex-info \"Cannot load segment: the id already holds different code\" {:segment seg})))\n      (assoc images seg image))))\n\n\n(defn- store-alias\n  [aliases image]\n  (let [address (:address image) seg (:segment image)]\n    (if-let [prior (find aliases address)]\n      (if (= (val prior) seg) aliases\n          (throw (ex-info \"Cannot load segment: the address is already aliased to another id\" {:address address :aliased-to (val prior)})))\n      (assoc aliases address seg))))\n\n\n(defn- admit-image\n  [machine v stamp opts]\n  (let [image (code/load-vector v stamp)\n        seg (or (get (:code-aliases machine) (:address image)) (:id opts)\n                (dec (vm/loaded-code-floor (:code machine))))]\n    (assoc image :segment seg :length (count v) :analysis (analysis/analyze v))))\n\n\n(defn load-vector\n  \"Validate and load a v4 vector; an existing address retains its local id.\"\n  ([machine v stamp] (load-vector machine v stamp {}))\n  ([machine v stamp opts]\n   (let [image (admit-image machine v stamp opts) seg (:segment image)]\n     (assoc machine :code (store-image (:code machine) image)\n            :code-aliases (store-alias (:code-aliases machine) image)\n            :program seg :control {:segment seg :pc 0} :window {} :k nil\n            :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil))))\n\n\n(defn attach-image\n  \"Attach a validated vector without changing execution fields.\"\n  [machine v stamp]\n  (let [image (admit-image machine v stamp {})]\n    (assoc machine :code (store-image (:code machine) image)\n           :code-aliases (store-alias (:code-aliases machine) image))))\n\n\n(defn load-ast\n  \"Project AST datoms through the phase-3 loader.\"\n  [machine datoms stamp]\n  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract stamp)\n  (load-vector machine (:vector (linearize/project-datoms datoms)) code/contract))\n\n\n(extend-type SemanticVM\n  vm/IVM\n  (step [machine] (telemetry/emit-snapshot\n                    (if (engine/ready-for-ingress? machine) machine (vm-hot machine 1)) :step))\n  (run [machine] (ffi/maybe-run machine register-run-scheduler))\n  (eval [machine ast]\n    (when ast (throw (ex-info \"The semantic VM executes loaded vectors; use load-ast\" {:ast ast})))\n    (engine/restore-initial-env (:env machine) (vm/run machine)))\n  (reset [machine]\n    (assoc machine :control (when-let [seg (:program machine)] {:segment seg :pc 0})\n           :window {} :k nil :value nil :halted? (nil? (:program machine)) :blocked? false))\n  (halted? [machine] (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? machine))\n  (blocked? [machine] (engine/vm-blocked? machine))\n  (value [machine] (engine/vm-value machine))\n  vm/IVMState\n  (control [machine] (:control machine))\n  (environment [machine] (:env machine))\n                                   (receive-module s m (dissoc dep :bindings))))\n                               received\n                               (linked-entries (:modules vm)))]\n                (-> received\n                    (update :installs dissoc module-name)\n                    (wake-installed module-name module-name nil))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- advance-install\n  \"Step the install child of `module-name` in this round, beside every\n   other task (section 7.3): `running` runs it until it halts or parks.\n   A parked child stays `parked`; a halted one goes on to `validated`; an\n   error refuses the install. An error while publishing -- attaching an\n   origin image, lowering the slice or a store snapshot, or receiving a\n   dependency -- refuses it too, at phase `:linked`, from the state the\n   round started with: nothing half-published survives, every waiter is\n   restored with the refusal, and no throw leaves the round.\n\n   Under a custody gate the child is stamped with the root's mode before\n   it runs; in `:exporting` and `:ended` it does not run.\"\n  [state module-name]\n  (let [inst (get-in state [:installs module-name])\n        mode (vm/gate-mode state)\n        [child refusal]\n        (when-not (contains? #{:exporting :ended} mode)\n          (try [(vm/run (stamp-gate (:vm inst) mode)) nil]\n               (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                 [nil (error-refusal module-name e)])))]\n    (cond\n      (and (nil? child) (nil? refusal)) state\n      refusal (refuse-install state module-name refusal)\n      (:blocked? child)\n      (assoc-in state [:installs module-name]\n                (assoc inst :vm child :phase :parked))\n      (:halted? child)\n      (try (link-install state module-name (assoc inst :vm child))\n           (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n             (refuse-install state module-name\n                             (assoc (error-refusal module-name e)\n                                    :phase :linked))))\n      :else (assoc-in state [:installs module-name]\n                      (assoc inst :vm child :phase :running)))))\n\n\n(defn- advance-installs\n  [state]\n  (reduce advance-install state (sort-by str (keys (:installs state)))))\n\n\n(defn- start-install\n  \"`loading`: spawn the child, or refuse on a loader defect. Returns\n   `[state refusal]`.\"\n  [state module-name response link-id]\n  (try\n    (let [[child state] (spawn-child state module-name response)]\n      [(assoc-in state [:installs module-name]\n                 {:phase :running, :vm child, :parent link-id,\n                  :response response})\n       nil])\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n      [state (assoc (error-refusal module-name e) :phase :loading)])))\n1458:(defn- advance-install\n1494:(defn- advance-installs\n1496:  (reduce advance-install state (sort-by str (keys (:installs state)))))\n1627:(defn- poll-links\n1643:        advance-installs)))\n2125:(defn check-wait-set\n2168:      (let [state (poll-links state\n2315:(defn scheduler-round\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
