{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_78","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '650,775p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '650,680p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '752,825p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '435,478p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '780,803p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; head -45 docs/agents/known-gaps.md; head -45 docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"the lanes agree with each other and with the golden vector and address\nfor every corpus program, and malformed input is refused by the\nvalidator on the direct vector (one refusal row per rule). Equivalence\nof a vector→datoms projection is retired, not deferred.\n\n## 4. Safepoints and the continuation format\n\n### 4.1 The safepoint table (UCF §7.4.1, rewritten)\n\nA safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic\nkinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n\nEvery wait is one of the two closed states of §2.4 (`act` with\n`{:deliver :rd}` or `tail` with `{:deliver :return}`); an effectful\ntail call raises the `tail` form of whichever row its effect falls in.\n`saved` is `saved(pc+1, rd)` of §2.4.\n\n| Safepoint kind | Raised by | State | Delivery |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| blocked read | `:stream-next rd c` → `blocked` | `act`, pending cursor cell `c` | `rd` ← read value; nil on `end`; `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n| blocked write | `:stream-put rd s v` → `full` | `act`, pending retains `W[v]` and the stream | `rd` ← written value on retry `ok` |\n| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call rd op args` → `ok` | `act`, pending `:ffi` (call id, response cursor cell); in-VM `:reason :next` with `:call-id` | `rd` ← correlated `ok` value; `error` raises; bookkeeping removed |\n| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `full` | `act`, pending `:ffi-request` (envelope verbatim); in-VM `:reason :put` with `:request-sent` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes the sent phase |\n| effectful call, non-tail | `:call rd f args false` whose operator yields a blocking effect | `act`, pending per the effect kind | per the kind's row |\n| effectful call, tail | `:call rd f args true` likewise | `tail`, pending per the effect kind | **through return** against `K` |\n| link request | `:module/require` miss, request in hand | `act` or `tail`, pending `:link-request` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes link response |\n| link response | request appended; polling for the correlated response | same state, pending `:link-response` | none at this phase; a matching `ok` makes it install; refusal raises |\n| install | the child runs | same state, pending `:install` + the body's install entry | on `linked`: the module symbol to `rd` or through return; on `refused`: raises |\n| halt | `:halt r`, or `:return r` with empty `K` | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/result` travels |\n\nTwo cases sit outside the table: an **explicit park** (`:park rd`) is a\n**no-wait safepoint**: it is a migration safepoint like every row\nabove, but it raises no wait entry; its `act` record is the body's\nparked record and a body of kind `:parked` names it by id (UCF §7.4.3\nr5, \"explicit park is the no-wait shape\"). A **reified continuation**\n(`:current-continuation`) is the one thing here that is *not* a\nsafepoint: it is a captured value, never a task state.\nWhole-task quiescence (empty ready queue, UCF §7.4.1) and the existing\nrefusal conditions (unsupported observation states, pending close) are\nunchanged.\n\nThe \"effectful call, tail\" row is the one place the rewrite is more\nthan notation: in the stack machine a tail effect's resume value landed\nin `val` and the body's `:return` then delivered it; here the body has\nno activation to resume into, so §2.4's `tail` state routes delivery\nthrough `K`. Write-result and through-return delivery are distinguished\nby the closed `deliver` record.\n\n### 4.2 The UCF frame\n\n`:yin.k/frame` (UCF §7.4.1) becomes, for the semantic profile:\n\n```clojure\n;; an act state (write-result delivery)\n{:yin.k/state    :act\n :yin.k/segment  A\n :yin.k/pc       n                       ; the resume pc, already pc+1\n :yin.k/reason   …                       ; unchanged\n :yin.k/window   {r (encoded) …}         ; saved(pc, rd), sorted by r\n :yin.k/deliver  {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n :yin.k/env      {sym encoded …}         ; E, unchanged\n :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return\n                    :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n                    :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/window {…} :yin.k/rd r} … ]\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}                    ; unchanged, §7.4.3\n\n;; a tail state (through-return delivery)\n{:yin.k/state    :tail\n :yin.k/segment  A :yin.k/site n         ; the tail call site, for provenance only\n :yin.k/reason   …\n :yin.k/deliver  {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k        [ … ]                   ; the frames delivery will pop\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}\n```\n\n`:yin.k/val`, `:yin.k/stack` and `:stack-base` are gone. The window's\n**membership is validated**, not trusted: the receiver recomputes\n`saved(pc, rd)` from A (§8.1) and refuses a window with a register\noutside it (`:window-extra`), missing from it (`:window-missing`), or\ncontaining the delivery destination (`:window-self`). Those three are\nthe membership diagnostics; the full frame validator also checks\nrepresentation (integer ids, sorted unique keys), body scope (every\nregister of the window belongs to the body `pc` is in), site/pc pairing\n(`pc` is a safepoint successor of the right kind for `reason`;\n`site` is a tail call), destination equality (`rd` is the `rd` of the\ninstruction at `pc−1`), delivery mode (`:act` carries `:rd`, `:tail`\ncarries `:return` and no window or env), return-frame sites (each\n`:return` frame's `pc` follows a non-tail call whose `rd` it names, and\nits window is `saved(pc, rd)` of that body), `K` structure (frames\ninnermost last, each well-formed), and pending compatibility with\n`reason`. Two captures of equal state therefore encode identically, and\ndead values never travel.\n\nThe validator is the pure function `(defect frame code context task)`\nof `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §3: `task` is decoded\ntask context (the parked key, the live FFI call ids, the free\nenvironment), never an engine; its rules are ordered, structural checks\nfirst, current-window rules on `act` only, return windows separately,\nand every `E` is checked to agree with the task's free environment off\nthe lexical names; it runs after canonical numbering, as the carrier\norders it.\n\nThe row carrier (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.2) changes\naccordingly: the frame rows are `:act [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nk-cref rd]` and `:tail [segment-A site k-cref]`, the kind being the\ndelivery discriminant, so `deliver` is not a slot; `:window` is a keyed\nrow `[r₁ V₁ r₂ V₂ …]` sorted by `r` in which a nil-valued register is a\npresent `[:lit nil]`; `:kframe` is `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nrd]`; `:stack` and `stack-base` are removed; the root gains `free-env`,\nthe task's initial free environment (continuation design §2, §6.3).\nThat amendment is applied to the carrier draft with this design's\nphase 6 (§10; `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §10.3).\n\n### 4.3 Foreign engines (UCF §7.4.2)\n\nA foreign engine's static safepoint map keeps `:yin.safepoint/segment`,\n`:yin.safepoint/pc` and `:yin.safepoint/engine`, replaces\n`:stack-effect` with the per-pc **def and use sets** and `L(pc)` derived\nfrom A (§8.1), and keeps `:yin.safepoint/layout` as a map from physical\nlocation to virtual register. The reconstruction obligation is: for an\n`act` state, produce the segment `A` and resume `pc`, every register in\n`saved(pc, rd) = L(pc) − {rd}` (never the not-yet-produced\ndestination), `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, the\ndelivery record, the pending state, and the captured store context; for\na `tail` state, produce the segment and the tail `site`, `K` with its\nvalidated frames, the delivery record and the pending state, and **no**\nsnapshot variant and every retry of the same park (§7.7.2). A re-emission\nafter a failed publication reuses the occurrence; it never mints a second\nrunnable checkpoint.\n\n## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\n\n### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\n\nSuperseded for the register-shaped machine by\n`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1 (two state columns, `act` and\n`tail`, and the `deliver` record); the table below describes the\nstack-shaped machine, which left at that design's phase 8 (2026-10-15),\nand the stack-shaped halves of the old `yin.vm.ucf` that served it —\nthe safepoint table, `stack-effect`, `parking-kinds` — were deleted\nwith it, un-ported (8f-1 ruling R2); the register machine's safepoint\nfacts live in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §4 and the frame validator of\n`yin.vm.semantic.frame`.\n\nThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\nInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\ninside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\nthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\nUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\nlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the\nreference machine at which the machine parks. The first draft's table\npresented itself as complete while omitting the parks that arrive through\n`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\ninstruction effect does, through `apply-call` → `handle-effect` →\n`call-park-entries`, and both ordinary and tail calls reach that path\n(`yin.vm.semantic`, `apply-call`). The complete set:\n\n|                | stream for the correlated id           |           |                        | error                                            |\n+----------------+----------------------------------------+-----------+------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n| :install       | a matching ok response, its            | pc+1      | St minus the popped    | the required module's symbol on                  |\n|                | obligations discharged, while          |           | operator and args      | linked; the refusal as the                       |\n|                | the install child runs its             |           |                        | effect's error on refused                        |\n|                | phases                                 |           |                        |                                                  |\n+----------------+----------------------------------------+-----------+------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+\n\nThe pending variants of the three reasons are in section 7.4.3.\n\n**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\n(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\nempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\nconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n\n### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\n\nThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\ncanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\nimage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\nof its own choosing, and the first draft promised it reconstruction data\nthat is not a function of the code: the saved window's values, the\nenvironment's bindings and the depth of `K` at a pc depend on the\n*activation*; the same pc is legitimately reached under many windows and\nenvironments.\nNone of that is derivable, and none of it needs to be: **the frame carries\nit.** E, the saved window, and every K frame's window, `rd` and env\ntravel in the value, as does the task's free environment; a foreign\nengine lowers the canonical frame directly.\n\nWhat *is* a function of the canonical vector alone, and what such an engine\nmust publish — **per segment and per engine profile**, derived from the\ncanonical stream at its own load time — is the static half:\n\n```clojure\n[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment      :segment/blake3-3f1a…]  ; the code, by address\n [sp :yin.safepoint/engine       :wasm32/v1]             ; the engine profile that produced this layout\n [sp :yin.safepoint/pc           5]                      ; canonical resume pc\n [sp :yin.safepoint/def          #{…}]                   ; registers defined at this pc (static, from A)\n [sp :yin.safepoint/use          #{…}]                   ; registers read at this pc (static, from A)\n [sp :yin.safepoint/live         #{…}]                   ; L(pc) (static, from A)\n [sp :yin.safepoint/lexically-required [x conn]]         ; names this pc can read from E (static)\n [sp :yin.safepoint/layout       {…}]]                   ; engine-owned: physical location → virtual register (continuation design §4)\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- `def`, `use`, `live` and `lexically-required` are **derived from the\n  canonical vector alone** (`yin.vm.semantic.analysis`). They are\n  identical for every engine and checkable against the reference machine\n  by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and\n  compares the saved window's key set with `live − {rd}` and the E-reads.\n  Window values, environments, `K` and physical layouts are **not**\n  claimed, not derived, and not in this table; they are the frame's\n  business.\n- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\n  keeps so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a UCF\n  value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\n- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\n  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\n  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\n  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\n- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\n  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\n  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\n  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\n  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\n  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\n  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\n\nSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \"where can this program be\nmigrated?\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\nthe safepoint set by construction.\n   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   free-env-cref    ; a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (continuation §6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (continuation §6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, see below\n```\n\n(Root replaced 2026-10-11 by continuation §2.6: `free-env-cref` added,\n`contract` is the one profile map, `code` holds `\"v4\"` canonical vectors,\n`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\nthose were the positional profiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n\nModule-pins ruling (2026-10-16): `requires` always carries BOTH\n`:yin.k/modules` and `:yin.k/host-exports`, using `{}` when empty, just as\nexisting requires collections are emitted when empty. A missing pin-map key\nis `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`; omission is not an alternative empty\nencoding. The module map records registry binding addresses (nil for host\nmodules); the host-export map records profile keywords for used qualified\nexports only. The module map is `{module-symbol address-keyword-or-nil}`:\ncarry `(:address entry)` unchanged and compare with `=`. The earlier ruling's\nstring-or-nil wording was a typing slip; linked entries hold Jing segment\nkeywords. The host-export map is `{qualified-symbol profile-keyword}`.\nBoth maps are validated during decode and dependency checking, including\nsymbol key cardinality. Malformed or absent maps refuse `:yin.k/undecodable\n:requires`. After custody, `check-modules!` checks every task before the first\nattach: terminal `:module-pin-mismatch` or `:host-export-mismatch` wins,\nthen one `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/discovery :complete` collects\nmissing pins and unadmitted install children under `:yin.k/missing`.\n\n\nKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\nparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\nnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\nneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\nnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\nordered waits.\n   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\n   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\n   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\n   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\n   safe.\n4. **Hashes.** **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Before digest verification and numbering, a parsed non-BLAKE3 content row id is refused as `:yin.k/undecodable :row-algorithm`, then each remaining row id is verified under its named algorithm, with malformed ids and digest mismatches refused as `:yin.k/hash-mismatch :row`.\n   Every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n   code key.\n5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n   lose a binding on lower.\n6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\n   root and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n   `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the\n   tie refusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\n# Known-gaps ledger and the standing review rule (Q6)\n\nAdopted by the owner 2026-10-14 (mob consolidation Q6).\n\n## Review rule\n- Stop on satisfied gates, not a round count.\n- The reviewer is a different model family from the author.\n- Reviewer prompts quote the slice row and its acceptance lines.\n- Blocking is P0 or P1, or a P2 in claimed acceptance, inside the slice's scope.\n- Fix rounds may not add public entry points, catalog ids or frontends.\n- A finding in code the slice should not have written means revert, not fix.\n- A disputed in-scope P1 goes to a third-family Architect, not a third engineer round.\n\n## Ledger\n| gap | evidence | owner seat | closing slice | severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| absent is permitted; a host entry under a pinned name refuses with `:linked nil` | fable L-d review P3-2 / L-d-2 | linker | open | P3 |\n| `semantic.scan-test` (then `semantic-register.scan-test`) C4 golden mismatch (JVM, Node) | slow tier at 355bd84b | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed 2026-10-14: the `[:c4 :py]` scanner golden regenerated mechanically after the other seat's linker relocation | P2 |\n| JVM `hash-registry-contract-test` sweep flags `ucf/rows.cljc` `decode*` | quick tier | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed 2026-10-14: the address-validation lint in `rows.cljc` repaired | P2 |\n| register design doc §2/§2.1 derivation prose still names the deleted resolve/lower-register stages | 8e Architect review P2-2 / 8g brief item 6 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in 8g: dated amendment in `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md` §2 (2026-10-15) | P3 |\n| native binder refusal collapsed to the projection's `:slot-kind` rule (reduced defence-in-depth coverage) | 8e review P2-3 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1, not applicable: native images carry no binders (closure = arity + body pc, derived from A; pins: opcode tables under contract 2/r2, `derive_test:179`, `debruijn_code_test:566`); the one binder validator is the projection's `:slot-kind` rule (`host_typed_values_test:242-247`); the native-side defence is the existing `:closure-arity` rule. | P2 |\n| 8d partial mappings (park beside a live reader wait; noise before the correlated FFI response) rest on bare-rows law-1 equality, not named successor tests | phase 8 final review P3-2 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1: `park-beside-a-live-reader-wait` and `noise-before-the-correlated-ffi-response` assert independent wake/resume after row handoff | P3 |\n| the slow-tier record \"22 of 22\" counts namespaces run by `~/ci/slow-tier.sh` (one JVM per namespace), not deftests; the tree has 231 `^:slow` deftests | phase 8 final review P3-4 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | recorded; Node slow C4 line on jupiter filed when it finishes | P3 |\n| `semantic/frame.cljc` (formerly :141; now ~:159) compares a captured frame's free-environment bindings with the task's free-env by host `=`; not NaN-reflexive on JS/Dart (a NaN-valued free binding in a captured/parked/waiting frame would be `:free-env-mismatch` there; same class as the 8f-3 NaN fix) | 8f-3 NaN ruling §5 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed on branch `srvm-frame-nan` (Architect signed off; the commit is the one adding `bindings=` to frame.cljc): guarded slotwise content equality; JVM focused 116 tests / 1380 assertions and quick tier 4007 tests / 249662 assertions green, host-equality mutant 9 failures; [evidence](../../collab/1792082835156-engineer-frame-nan.report.md) (Node/Dart lanes assigned to orchestrator) | P3 |\n| `ucf/rows.cljc` `decode*` (~:931-935) host-`distinct` clause on map/set keys: emit/decode asymmetry for 0.0/-0.0 keys on every host and a Dart-only refusal for 1/1.0 keys | frame-nan Architect review P2-1 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1 (round 2): map/set row keys refuse `:equality-collapse` under `cbor/equiv` on every host (emit, decode, abstract check); lower refuses `:host-collapse` when the host collection merged members; JVM verified, Node/Dart assigned to orchestrator | P2 |\n| dead fixture: `yin.vm.malformed-rows/malformed-vectors` (the v3 §7.5 vector rules) has no reader since the old vector validator died | 8g sweep | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1: deleted after source/test reader search | P3 |\n| what died at phase 8, for the record: `yin.vm.completion` (its agreement facts survive in `semantic/scan_test`), and `yin.vm.ucf`'s v3 canonicalizer and stack-shaped safepoint halves (un-ported: completion in 8f-1, UCF halves in 8f-3; R2/R3; `yin.vm.ucf` is now only `code-address`) | 8f-1 ruling | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | recorded in 8g (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` I-6, the dependency-completion banner, linker §5.2 note) | P3 |\n| Historical citations of `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` with v3 section numbers, now wrong after the rename and outside the nine-line allowance: `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:655` (§2), `src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc:67`, `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream.cljs:10` (§7), `src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd.cljd:6` (§8), `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/jfr_benchmark_analysis.md:5,268` (§8), `test/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc:3` (§8), `test/yin/repl_test.cljc:37` (§8), `test/yin/vm/semantic/linearize_test.cljc:286` (§2.7); in the renamed design §2 is the machine, §7 is \"What A is\", §8 is the derivations | 8g Architect review P2-1 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1: retargeted to the history block or current projection/cutover sections; only prose changed | P3 |\n| Historical source prose outside the nine citation-line allowance: the optimisation note mentions the deleted `vm/opcase`; `ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` says it replaces the now-deleted `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` | 8f-1 review D1 / P3-5 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1: optimisation note marks `vm/opcase` historical; rows inspector records deletion of the predecessor in 8f-1 | P3 |\n| Node `store-test` two-worker-threads flaky | quick and slow tiers | store | open | P3 |\n| Dart `dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` (Linux) | quick tier | dao.stream | open | P3 |\n| L-e (closure-origin and store-ownership verification) not implemented | docs 7.5 | linker | L-e | P1 |\n| DHT 64 KiB blob cap: `py` cannot travel over DHT | docs 7.5 | DHT seat | chunked transport | P2 |\n| CLI refusal for a bad `--dht-trust` value no