est/yin/vm/semantic/frame_test.cljc\n12\t0\ttest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc\n137\t17\ttest/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc\n28\t0\ttest/yin/vm/semantic/vm_test.cljc\n54\t20\ttest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\nLoading initial Timbre config from: :default\nWARNING: load already refers to: #'clojure.core/load in namespace: yin.vm.encoder, being replaced by: #'yin.vm.encoder/load\n      16 (s/let1 'k1 capture (s/let1 'k2 capture\n                                     (cat-of (cat-of (cat-of (s/lit []) (s/v 'k1)) (s/v 'k2)) read)))\n      18 (s/app (s/lam ['p] {:type :vm/park}) (s/lit 1))\n      19 (s/lit 42)\n      21 (s/let1 'f (s/lam [] (s/v 'x)) (s/app (s/lam ['x] (s/app (s/v 'f) read)) (s/lit 1)))\n      22 (s/app (s/lam ['p 'q] (cat-of (cat-of (s/lit []) (s/v 'p)) (s/v 'q))) (s/lit nil) read))))\n\n\n(defn- attach-reader\n  [m ring]\n  (let [[ref m] (engine/attach-resource m ring)\n        [id m] (engine/gensym m \"cursor\")]\n    (engine/handle-cursor m {:stream ref} id)))\n\n\n(defn reference-machines\n  []\n  (into\n    (mapv (fn [[n [label wrap]]]\n            (let [ring (s/ring 4)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_84","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '560,570p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; rg -n '\"'^## 2|''^### 1.3|''^### 7.4|''^### 5.7|''^### 8.6'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1700,1708p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1780,1798p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '455,466p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_84","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '560,570p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; rg -n '\"'^## 2|''^### 1.3|''^### 7.4|''^### 5.7|''^### 8.6'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1700,1708p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1780,1798p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '455,466p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 7. Eligibility and safepoint validation\n\n### 7.1 Whole-task requirements\n\nOnly blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine\nexport boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth\nkind. All ready queues are empty, all installs complete in the UCF sense,\nand root/child state is stable. Refuse any root or child with an observe\nwait, unapplied held observation, reachable unminted cursor, pending close,\nor link request without its installed response cursor. These are D4-D9\nholds, not occasions to invent a new pending variant or opcode.\n255:### 1.3 States between instructions, and the task product.** The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks by itself. They are special cases of its general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect; atomicity of a step is not transactionality of the outside world (an append performed before a host exception is not undone). A task is a **product**: at most one active activation (the `run`), its ordered pending waits, its ordered completed wakes (the ready queue: a woken wait with the value, status and cursor update its effect produced, carried by the `:ready` row of §2.3), its parked table, and its stores. The scheduler constructs every combination: resuming one of several woken waits leaves the others queued and the unresolved ones pending (`engine.cljc:2294-2312`, `:2305`). Every such state is **representable**. The lift refuses only what is not **portable** (a value with no wire form), not **satisfied** (code or dependencies the receiver lacks), or a **hold** (a driver-internal observation in flight: §7.1 step 1), never a pc. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc of a body: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`), and the stack kernel's operand region at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)` above a dead prefix (§4.3); both are checks (§5.6), not theorems. A root whose task has an active activation has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\n258:## 2. The row carrier\n1526:### 5.7 The stepping driver\n1999:### 7.4 Fuel\n2416:### 8.6 Slice 6d-a: reference lane\npositions and cannot be used), and refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless\n`:complete`, `:yin.k/trees` included; number cursor cells (draft §6); walk\nwaits, parked records, the store slice, module stores, the free\nenvironment (from the kernel's `:free-env` field or the lift's explicit\n`:free-env` argument, §6.3, never from a current `E`), the result,\ninstall children; run `frame/defect` over every\nframe; emit rows and the census. A de Bruijn source walks the frames\n`layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail` reconstruct.\n\n   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\nModule-pins ruling (2026-10-16): `requires` always carries BOTH\n`:yin.k/modules` and `:yin.k/host-exports`, using `{}` when empty, just as\nexisting requires collections are emitted when empty. A missing pin-map key\nis `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`; omission is not an alternative empty\nencoding. The module map records registry binding addresses (nil for host\nmodules); the host-export map records profile keywords for used qualified\nexports only. The module map is `{module-symbol address-keyword-or-nil}`:\ncarry `(:address entry)` unchanged and compare with `=`. The earlier ruling's\nstring-or-nil wording was a typing slip; linked entries hold Jing segment\nkeywords. The host-export map is `{qualified-symbol profile-keyword}`.\nBoth maps are validated during decode and dependency checking, including\nsymbol key cardinality. Malformed or absent maps refuse `:yin.k/undecodable\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
