kind` does not split on `/`); decode already refuses the numeric `equiv` collapses | follow-up-1 round-2 Architect confirm P3 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | open: mirror the `built` count guard in `keyed`, one test on Node (JS-only case) | P3 |\n| Closed 2026-10-16: Q5 receiver module and used host-export pins | Architect module-pins Part B; I-8 | VM Runtime | landed as module-pins commit 2 (Architect signed off 2026-10-17): exact keyword addresses and profile equality, complete collected refusal before attach | P1 |\n| R4: carried install child bypasses receiver admission and carries sender evidence | fetch threat model §4.4 | VM Runtime | landed as module-pins commit 1 (origin/master ce76c846): explicit `:yin.k/admitted-modules` name/address gate before attach; lift never carries sender admission fields; a carried admission field refuses `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response`; the pin carrier is module-pins commit 2 | P1 |\n| Composition fetch loop per-run lease and budget absent | fetch threat model §4.3; reserved `:lease` policy | composition | outside module-pins slice; no fetch loop added | P2 |\n| Closed 2026-10-16: holder driver forwards admitted-modules | Architect module-pins review P2-1 | VM Runtime | config forwarded to lowering and fenced recovery; parked-installer regression covers absent and admitted config | P2 |\n\n| the lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc` / `lift-task*`) and a queued completed wake, the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc` / `run-cycle`) and loses a computed boundary on tenure loss (`step-active`), the custody kind tables list three kinds, and the stack layout is undefined at jump pcs (`layout.cljc` / `stack-layouts`): the carrier cannot express, and the holder cannot pause at, an arbitrary pc | owner invariant 2026-10-17; Architect plan v2.1 `collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md` | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e; acceptance pending | P1 |\n| ledger checkpoint 2 | `:segment/blake3-f3c7c3c2d3c9c7c64b10b4cd46f711df1befd76bd366f03a40cc157db3ce0d34` | `:segment/blake3-5e240ede63c00f163d700e431c4e09762428f1b0de4ed48e5fea0fbf50b90d92` |\n| ledger checkpoint 3 | `:segment/blake3-c31a1847cd8466aafe6220fca1972b72924a6c69eef3e1260970619ec80d787d` | `:segment/blake3-51ca5000cf870c9bd7c2c284dc8a8a38734ef48fc3e3ebd48fe525943d34bfb9` |\n| ledger checkpoint 4 | `:segment/blake3-ab97dc5502245c63ab49b45088b88989fa143616cadf6e298b8ae90d9694d711` | `:segment/blake3-42b4357517e46880bcf804e32657f483088d0b5821b36249cdd38aa110afb79e` |\n\nLedger projection digest: `2b26e75e8969428761c4f7813d1ffc886d30d160f2f3e8f80076bbe24ef1f227` → `419e3aa652d79cab6921cf7a6c02972bcfe2d3b88ba472bc03ac8f1a935f27d2`.\n\n**I-9 — 2026-10-16, any-PC.** The current lift rejects active control and queued completed outcomes, while the holder runs unrestricted computation. Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the carrier to run states and complete scheduler products. 6d-a includes completed stream/FFI/link/install outcomes, cursor-update encoding, bounded bridge-aware fuel and closed-gate execution guards. 6d-b adds explicit state retention on tenure failure and holder migration from fuel boundaries. 6e extends backend layouts and transfer laws. Earlier static-safepoint-only lift prescriptions are historical. No compatibility promise is added.\n              :branch-false (recur machine seg (if (get W (nth inst 1)) (inc pc) (nth inst 2)) W E K remaining)\n              :define (let [value (get W (nth inst 3))\n                            next-state (engine/put-active machine\n                                                          (engine/env-store-of E) (nth inst 2) value)]\n                        (recur next-state seg (inc pc) (assoc W (nth inst 1) value) E K remaining))\n              :gensym (let [[id next-state] (engine/gensym machine (nth inst 2))]\n                        (recur next-state seg (inc pc) (assoc W (nth inst 1) id) E K remaining))\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the order as `lift-task*` runs it (B4),\nand the discharge rule.** The paragraph above put the dependency closure\nbefore the numbering and the walk, but the carried segments, contexts\nand module stores are known only once the walk has found them, so the\nclosure cannot run first.\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: `lift-task` now takes `:header` and runs custody checks after the walk.\nThe header is nil for a fork; otherwise it contains occurrence,\narbitration, next-op-seq, optional origin and the local `:yin.k/enrolled`\nset. That set never enters the pack. The linker/composition supplies\n`:source-index` as `{A #{tree-address}}`; code and trees use its existing\nfetch and admission path, with A the only code identity and the AST\nretained. The holder retains serve answers by `[task-path resource-id]`;\nencode uses those answers without serving externally.\n\nThe merged order is:\n\n**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n\n1. **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observe wait, held entry, unminted cursor, pending close or link request without its cursor. These are driver-owned incomplete transitions. Their completion can depend on external acknowledgement or backpressure; there is no one-cycle resolution guarantee. There is no ready-queue quiescence refusal: completed outcomes travel in order.\n2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked table holds; else `:blocked` when the wait set or the ready queue is non-empty; else `:halted` with empty wait set and queue; anything else is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (unreachable by construction).\n3. **Free environment** (§6.3): the option, else the kernel field, else\n   `:missing-free-env`.\n4. **The walk.** The active run state with its window trimmed to `L(pc)`, waits (state and pending), ordered ready outcomes, the parked table, the whole\n   store, the result (halted only), install children (each a recursive\n   `lift-task*` in the child role with its own accumulator), then the\n   module stores by reachability. Every value is classified `[:lit x]`,\n   `[:row n]` or `[:cursor-cell source]`; references are authenticated\n   against the kernel's resource table before encoding\n   (`:forged-resource-reference`), a foreign-owned value is\n| `:ffi` | `C, call-id-D, op-D` | Completed FFI response reader; restore engine reason `:next` and correlation fields. |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D` | Completed request append; restore `:request-sent true`, so successful resume creates the response wait rather than delivering the append result to the guest. |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D` | Completed link-request outcome; no outstanding request or response transport is required. |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D` | Completed link-response outcome. |\n| `:install` | `module-S, try?-D` | Completed initializer outcome; no remaining install entry is required. |\n\n\n## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\n\nCursor-cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\ntwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cursor cell's number must depend on\neverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\nelsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\nholding cursor cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\nlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\ncan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\ntopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\nwhich any algorithm is checked.\n\n**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering is\ndefined as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\n\nEvery UCF lift or lower operation returns one map dispatching on\n`:yin.k/status`; the set is closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries\nthe data needed to act on it. Admission outcomes (below) are the second\nfamily, disjoint from this one.\n\n| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\n| `:yin.k/refused` | lift | `:yin.k/reason`, the export or abort refusal |\n| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\nTesting dao.stream.cbor-test\n\nTesting dao.stream.chunks-test\n\nTesting dao.stream.datagram.jvm-test\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_105","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '469,500p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; rg -n 'Kind rules|\"'^'\"\\\\| root|\"'^'\"\\\\| \"'`:task`|:task'\"\\\\s|:act\\\\s' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | head -15; sed -n '813,831p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '1278,1296p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; rg -n 'all tasks|No \"'`:yin.k|three kinds|blocked or parked root'\"' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
